[Prop 21 (Metric distortion of rectified triangulation) of arXiv:2607.27535, 'Universal topological statistics on \\ triangulated singular spaces'] Let $\phi: K \rightarrow M$ be a $C^2$ triangulation of a compact set $M \subset \RR^D$ and let $\psi: \widetilde{K} \rightarrow M$ be its rectification. Then for every pair of points $x, y \in \widetilde{K}$, the following holds if $\alpha\beta < 1$:
    \begin{align} 
        \frac{\dist_{M} (\psi(x), \psi(y) )}{\dist_{\widetilde{K}} (x, y)} \in \bigg[ \frac{1-\alpha \beta}{1+\alpha \beta}, \>\> \frac{1+\alpha \beta}{1-\alpha \beta} \bigg]
    \end{align}
    where $\alpha, \beta$ are given as follows\footnote{Note that $\beta$ depends on simplices of $K$, not simplices of $\widetilde K$.}:
    \begin{align*}
        \alpha = \frac{\sup_{x \in K} \| \hess_x \phi \|_{\mathsf{op}}}{\inf_{x \in K} \sing_{\min}(\Diff_x \phi)}, \quad \beta = \sqrt{d}\cdot \max_{\sigma \in K}  \frac{\diam(\sigma)^2}{\sthick(\sigma)}
    \end{align*}

