[Lemma eq:Bsup of arXiv:2510.24424, 'On the Fourier Coefficients of Critical Gaussian Multiplicative Chaos'] Let $B_t$ be a standard linear Brownian motion. Then there exists a constant $C>0$ such that for any $t>e$, $a>0$,
    \begin{align}
        \mathbb{P}\Big(\sup_{s\in [e,t]}B_s\leq a\Big) \leq C\frac{(a+1)}{\sqrt{t}+1}.
    \end{align}
    Furthermore, if $a,b>0$ and $\mathbb{P}_{(0,a)}^{(t,b)}$ denotes the law of a Brownian bridge of length $t>e$ from $a$ to $b$, then
    \begin{align}
        \mathbb{P}_{(0,a)}^{(t,b)}\Big(B_s\geq 0,\forall s\in [e,t]\Big) \leq C \frac{(a+1)(b+1)}{t+1}.
    \end{align}

[Thm 1 (Strong failure of regularity persistence) of arXiv:2609.02523, '\bf Strong Ill-Posedness in critical and subcritical regimes for the Hunter--Saxton equati'] For any $s\in (1, \nicefrac {3}{2}]$, there exists an initial datum
\begin{equation*}
  u_0\in L^\infty(\R) \cap \dot H^1(\R) \cap \dot H^{s}(\R)
\end{equation*}
for which the corresponding unique global dissipative solution $u$ of \eqref{HS} satisfies
\begin{equation}
  \sup_{0<t<T}\|u(t,\cdot)\|_{\dot H^{s}}=\infty,
  \qquad\text{for every }T>0.
\end{equation}
In particular, $u\notin C([0,T];\dot H^{s}(\R))$ for every $T>0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

