[Corollary 5 (Quantitative passage toward the subcritical cutoff) of arXiv:2608.20638, 'Provable Edge-of-Stability for Adam on a One-Dimensional Quadratic'] Assume the hypotheses of Proposition~\ref{prop:Psi-decay} and
$w_{\max}>4$.  Fix $0<\delta<\overline W$ and $T\in\Nzero$, and let
\begin{equation}
    \tau:=\min\{n\in\Nzero:w_{T+n}>\overline W-\delta\}.

\end{equation}
If $w_T>\overline W-\delta$, then $\tau=0$.  Otherwise, set
\begin{equation}
    q:=1-\delta\!\left(
       \frac{c\eta}
       {\sqrt{\max\{v_T,\Psi_T\}}+c\eta/4},
       \overline W-\delta
       \right).

\end{equation}
Then
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
    \tau\le 1+\Bigg\lceil
    \frac{2}{1-\max\{\beta_2,q\}}
    \log\Bigg[
       \frac{16}{c^2\eta^2}
       \left(
          v_T+
          \frac{2(1-\beta_2)}{1-\max\{\beta_2,q\}}\Psi_T
       \right)
    \Bigg]
    \Bigg\rceil.
\end{aligned}

\end{equation}

[Proposition prop:diffusion+jumpType of arXiv:2412.05030, 'Characterization of Subordinate Symmetric Markov Processes'] Assume $(\mathcal{E},\mathcal{F})$ satisfies the diffusion+jump type heat kernel estimate HK$_-(\phi_c,\phi_j)$ and 
  let $S$~ be an independent subordinator whose Bernstein function is given in ((eq:Bernstein function of the paper))
  with $\phi := \phi_c \wedge \phi_j$.
  Then, the jump kernel $J(x,y)$ of the subordinate process satisfies 
  the following estimate: 
  \begin{equation}
    J(x,y) \simeq \frac{1}{V(x,r)}\left(\frac{1}{\psi(r)} +\overline{\phi}(\phi_j(r)^{-1})\right),
  \end{equation}
  where $r := d(x,y)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

