body:
[Conjecture 3 of arXiv:2511.04938, 'Uniform dimension theorems for parabolic SPDEs'] The uniform dimension theorem \eqref{eq:HEAT:torus} is valid whenever $p \ge 2$ provided that
	the set $\{v \in \R^p:\, \inf_{\|x\|=1}
	\|\sigma(v)x\|=0\}$ is polar for $u$. (Stated as a conjecture by the paper; open unless the paper says otherwise.)

[Quest of arXiv:2509.06730, 'Hyperbolic branching Brownian motion: \\ the empirical limit measure'] In this paper, the underlying stochastic process from which we build the branching process is continuous in time and space. It is also natural to consider a discrete setting, that is a branching random walk on a hyperbolic group. Let $\Gamma$ be a non--elementary hyperbolic group, generated by a finite set $S$. Given a probability measure $\nu$ on $S$ with $\ensuremath{\mathrm{supp} \ } \hspace{-3pt}(\nu) =S$, we can then construct a random walk on $\Gamma$ by setting $p(x,y)=\nu(x^{-1}y)$, and hence a branching random walk (BRW) with {branching} rate $\beta>0$. Let $\vert x \vert$ be the norm of $x\in \Gamma$ in the word metric induced by $S$. Then for the random walk induced by $\nu$, $(X_n,n\geq 0)$, there {are} $\sigma^2,v>0$ such that
    \begin{equation*}
            \frac{\vert X_n\vert}{n}\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{a.s.}v \qquad \text{and} \qquad
            \frac{\vert X_n\vert - nv}{\sqrt{\sigma^2n}} \xrightarrow[n\to \infty]{d} \mathcal{N}(0,1),
    \end{equation*}
    see for example [bjorklund_central_2010] and the references therein.
    That is, $(\vert X_n\vert)_{n\geq 0}$ satisfies a strong law of large numbers and a central limit theorem. Like the hyperbolic plane $\ensuremath{\mathbb{H}}$, $\Gamma$ can be endowed with a natural boundary $\partial \Gamma$ with metric $d_a$ where $a>1$ is the visual parameter. In this setting, we can again consider $\Lambda$, the set of accumulation points of the BRW on $\partial \Gamma$, and $\mu_\infty$, the limit of the empirical measure. There have been multiple works studying $\Lambda$ and its dimension, for example [sidoravicius_limit_2023], but none studying $\mu_\infty$. We believe that the methods from this paper {should} transfer easily to this setting, in particular because the LLN and CLT guarantee that an equivalent of Proposition (prop:conv_typical_to_mu of the paper) still holds true. We believe the following should be true. 

    \begin{conj}
        In this setting, $\dim  \mu_\infty = \left(\frac{\beta}{v\log(a)} \right)\wedge \dim \partial \Gamma$ almost surely. 
    \end{conj}

    Note that $\dim \partial \Gamma = \frac{\delta}{\log a}$ where $\delta$ is the exponential growth rate of the volume of $\Gamma$. This would be in contrast to the complicated expressions for $\dim \Lambda$, for example determined by [sidoravicius_limit_2023] and [hueter_anisotropic_2000]. (Stated as a question by the paper; open unless the paper says otherwise.)

[Proposition 10 of arXiv:2608.03497, 'Connective Constants on Nested Fractal Graphs'] For every \(a\in V_\infty\) and \(n\ge1\),
\begin{equation}
  c_{a,n}
  \le
  \sum_{q=1}^{\min\{Q,n\}}C^q
  \sum_{\substack{\ell_1+\cdots+\ell_q=n\\ \ell_i\ge1}}
  \prod_{i=1}^q c_{\ell_i}.

\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition eq:defTripleNorm of arXiv:2411.07157, 'Rough differential equations in the flow approach'] For every $N\geqslant1$, $\tau\in\mathcal{T}_*$ and $\text{$\mathfrak{z}$}^{\tau} \in {\mcb{C}}[\tau]$, and  $\mu\in(0,1]$, set
    \begin{equs}        K^{\otimes 1+\mathfrak{s}(\tau)}_{N,\mu}\text{$\mathfrak{z}$}^\tau(t,s^\tau)\eqdef \big(K_{N,\mu}\otimes\dots\otimes K_{N,\mu}\big)*\text{$\mathfrak{z}$}^\tau(t,s^\tau)\,.
    \end{equs}
We then endow $\text{$\mathfrak{z}$}^\tau$ with the norm
    \begin{equs}       \nnorm{\text{$\mathfrak{z}$}^\tau}_{N,\mu} \eqdef \norm{K^{\otimes1+\mathfrak{s}(\tau)}_{N,\mu}\text{$\mathfrak{z}$}^\tau}_{L^\infty_tL^1_{s^\tau}((-\infty,1]\times\R^{\mathfrak{s}(\tau)})}\equiv \sup_{t\leqslant1}\int_{\R^{\mathfrak{s}(\tau)}} |K^{\otimes1+\mathfrak{s}(\tau)}_{N,\mu} \text{$\mathfrak{z}$}^{\tau}(t,s^\tau)|\mathrm{d} s^\tau\,.\textcolor{white}{blabl}
    \end{equs}

[Theorem 4 of arXiv:2606.11142, 'Strong invariance principles for diffusions, Markov chains and their perturbations.'] Assume that conditions A1, A2, A3, A4 hold. Let $\mathbb{T}_n = \{t_1, \dots, t_n\}$, $\mathbb{T}_m = \{\tau_1, \dots, \tau_m\}$ be a micro and macro grids respectively, and $n = m^l$ for some $l > \frac{1}{\min(\gamma_2/2,\alpha_2,\beta)}$. Then, there exists a probability space supporting versions of the diffusion process $X_t$ and the Markov chain $Y_t$, and constants $C_1, C_2 > 0$ such that for every $1/2 < \alpha < 1$:
\begin{equation*}
\mathbb{P} \left( \sup_{t \in [0,1]} |X_t - Y_t| \ge 2m^{\alpha-1} + 2K_1m^{-1} \right) \le C_1 m \Delta^{(n)} + 4md \exp\left(-\frac{m^{2\alpha-1}}{2C_2 d}\right),
\end{equation*}
where $K_1$ is the upper bound for the norm of the drift and $Y_t$ is the linear interpolation of the Markov chain. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 71 of arXiv:2607.18104, '2607.18104'] Fix some $\ell \in \N$. 
Then there exist some constants $c,C>0$, depending only on $\ell$, such that for all $n\in \N$, we get that 
\begin{equation}
    \P_{\per}\left(C_{u,v}^{i,n,\ell} \, \forall (u;v)\in E_{\ell n} , i \in \lbr n\rbr \right) \geq 1-C\exp\big(-c n^{1/6} \big) . 
\end{equation}

