[Definition of arXiv:2607.02410, 'Polynomial mixing for polygonal side matchings'] Let $P$ be a $2n$-gon whose vertices are labelled from $1$ to $2n$ in a clockwise manner, and let $X$ be a chord diagram on $P$. The \textit{gluing graph} of $X$, denoted $G(X)$, is a directed graph whose vertices are the vertices of $P$ and whose edges are
	\begin{equation*}
		E = \bigcup_{\{\{x,x+1\}, \{y,y+1\}\} \in X } \{ (x, y+1), (y, x+1) \} \, ,
	\end{equation*}
	where the vertices $1$ and $2n+1$ are equivalent.

[Lemma 12 of arXiv:2506.09592, 'Extremal process of the local time of simple\\random walk on a regular tree'] For $n\ge1$,~$t>0$ and~$\lambda>0$, denote
\begin{equation}

\Gamma(\lambda):=\bigl\{x\in\BbbL_n\colon \sqrt{L_t(x)}\ge m_n-\lambda\bigr\}.
\end{equation}
Then for all~$\lambda>0$,
\begin{equation}

\lim_{k\to\infty}\,\limsup_{n\to\infty}\,P^\varrho\Bigl(\exists x,y\in\Gamma(\lambda)\colon y\in B_{n-k}(x)\smallsetminus B_k(x)\Bigr) = 0.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

