[Definition 9 of arXiv:2606.01742, 'Large deviation principles for SPDEs with locally Lipschitz coefficients'] (Large deviation principle) The solution $\{u^\varepsilon\}_{\varepsilon> 0}$ to  Eq.\,\eqref{SHE} is said to satisfy an LDP on $\mathcal E^{\beta}([0,T]\times K,\mathbb R)$ with the rate function $I$, if the following two conditions hold:

	(a) (Upper bound) For each closed subset $F$ of $\mathcal E^{\beta}([0,T]\times K, \mathbb R)$,
	\begin{align*}
		\limsup_{ \varepsilon\to0}  \varepsilon\log \mathbb{P}(u^\varepsilon\in F)\le -\inf_{z\in F} I(z).
	\end{align*}

	(b) (Lower bound) For each open subset $G$ of $\mathcal E^{\beta}([0,T]\times K, \mathbb R)$,
	\begin{align*}
		\liminf_{ \varepsilon\to0}  \varepsilon\log \mathbb{P}(u^\varepsilon\in G)\ge -\inf_{z\in G} I(z).
	\end{align*}

[Theorem Thm: Perc_Distance of arXiv:2608.00399, 'The Heavy-tailed Frog Model'] Let $d \ge 2$ and $p > p_c^{\mathrm{site}}(d)$, where $p_c^{\mathrm{site}}(d)$ is the critical parameter of Bernoulli site percolation on $\mathbb Z^d$. Then, there exists a constant $\rho = \rho(p, d) \in [1, \infty)$ such that 
\begin{align*}
	&\limsup_{\|y\|_1 \rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{\|y\|_1} \log \mathbb P_p( o \leftrightarrow y, d(o, y) > \rho \|y\|_1) < 0, \\
    &\; \text{ and } \; \; \limsup_{\ell \rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{\ell} \log \mathbb P_p( o \leftrightarrow y, d(o, y) > \ell ) < 0,
\end{align*}
where $d(x, y)$ is the graph distance between $x$ and $y$ on the percolation clusters. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma l4.7 of arXiv:2604.22510, 'Asymptotics of Multi-Scale McKean--Vlasov Diffusions with Super-Linear Kernels: a Lifted S'] Fix $K < \infty $. Consider any sequence $\{(\varphi^{k},{{\boldsymbol{ {\Pi}}}}^{k})\}_{k\in\mathbb{N}}$ such that for every $k\in \mathbb{N}$, $(\varphi^{k},{{\boldsymbol{ {\Pi}}}}^{k})$ is viable and
	\begin{equation}
		\int_{\mathbb{R}^d\times\mathbb{R}^m\times\mathscr{P}_2(\mathbb{R}^m)\times[0,T]}\big(|h|^2+|y|^4+M_4(\rho)\big){{\boldsymbol{ {\Pi}}}}^k(dhdyd\rho dt)\leq K.
	\end{equation}
	Then the limit $(\varphi,{{\boldsymbol{ {\Pi}}}})$ is a viable pair.

