body:
[Theorem thm:FG-common-noise-criterion of arXiv:2604.22453, 'Adapted Wasserstein Barycenters of Gaussian Processes'] The following statements are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] The common-noise input condition (equation eq:common-noise.input.condition of the paper) holds.
\item[(ii)] For every \(t\in[T]\), there exist orthogonal matrices \(\mathbf Q_t^1,\ldots,\mathbf Q_t^K\in\mathscr O(d)\) such that the matrix
\begin{equation}

(\mathbf P_t^\star)^{k,\ell}=\mathbf Q_t^k(\mathbf Q_t^\ell)^\intercal,
\qquad k,\ell\in[K],
\end{equation}
minimizes \(\Phi_t\) over \(\mathscr{P}_+(K,d)\).
\item[(iii)] There exists an unrestricted barycenter \(\overline{\mathbb{X}}\in\mathcal{FG}_K(T,d)\) constructed in Theorem~(thm:gaussian.multicausal.optimizer of the paper) which is \(\mathcal{AW}_2\)-equivalent to an element of \(\mathcal{FG}(T,d)\).
\item[(iv)] The unrestricted adapted barycenter problem admits a barycenter representative in \(\mathcal{FG}(T,d)\).
\end{enumerate}
When these equivalent conditions hold, the matrix \(\mathbf P^\star=\diag(\mathbf P_1^\star,\ldots,\mathbf P_T^\star)\in\mathscr{P}_+(K,T,d)\) minimizes \(\Phi\), and
$
\mathbb{G}^{
\accentset{\overline{\hphantom{a}}}{a}
,
\accentset{\overline{\hphantom{L}}}{L}
}\in \mathcal{FG}(T,d)
$
is an $\mathrm{FP}_2$-barycenter, where $
\accentset{\overline{\hphantom{L}}}{L}
\in\mathscr{L}(T,d)$ is defined by
\[

\accentset{\overline{\hphantom{L}}}{L}
_{\cdot,t}=\sum_{k=1}^K\lambda_kL_{\cdot,t}^k\mathbf Q_t^k,
\quad t\in[T].
\]
Moreover, if $
\accentset{\overline{\hphantom{\mathbb{X}}}}{\mathbb{X}}
:=\mathbb{G}_K^{
\accentset{\overline{\hphantom{a}}}{a}
,
\accentset{\overline{\hphantom{\mathbf L}}}{\mathbf L}
}$ denotes the barycenter representative built from the optimizers $\mathbf P_t^\star$ in Theorem~(thm:gaussian.multicausal.optimizer of the paper), then
\begin{equation}

\mathcal{AW}_2(
\accentset{\overline{\hphantom{\mathbb{X}}}}{\mathbb{X}}
,\mathbb{G}^{
\accentset{\overline{\hphantom{a}}}{a}
,
\accentset{\overline{\hphantom{L}}}{L}
})=0.
\end{equation}

