[Definition of arXiv:2608.10415, 'Continuous random variables'] A measurable space is a set $X$, given with a set of subsets $M\subset P(X)$, called measurable sets, which form an algebra, in the sense that:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\emptyset,X\in M$.

\item $E\in M\implies E^c\in M$.

\item $M$ is stable under countable unions and intersections.
\end{enumerate}

[Prp proposition2 of arXiv:2411.15724, 'Wasserstein Convergence Rates for Empirical Measures of Random Subsequence of $\{nα\}$'] Under the hypotheses of Theorem (TH1 of the paper),
\begin{equation} 
\mathbb E \big[\mathbb W_2^2(\mu_n,\mu) \big] =
\begin{cases}
O \big( \frac{1}{n} \big) , & \textrm{if } \beta \gamma < 2;\\
O\big( \frac{\log n}{n} \big), & \textrm{if } \beta \gamma =2;\\
O\big( \frac{1}{n^{2/(\beta \gamma)}} \big),& \textrm{if } \beta \gamma >2.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
Furthermore, if $\beta \gamma < 2$, then
\begin{align} 
\lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} n \mathbb E \big[\mathbb W_2^2(\mu_n,\mu) \big] = \frac{1}{4 \pi^2} \sum_{m \ne 0} \frac{1 - | \varphi(2\pi m \alpha)|^2}{m^2 |1 - \varphi(2\pi m \alpha)|^2}.
\end{align}

