[Theorem 8 (Duality for the process $X^{(N)}$) of arXiv:2606.00781, '2606.00781'] Assume that $\Lambda$ is not the zero measure. Then the process $X^{(N)}$ is dual to the process $Y^{(N)}$ with respect to the duality kernel $D^{(N)}:\{0,\ldots,N\}^2\to\rz$ defined via
   \begin{equation} 
      D^{(N)}(n,m)\ :=\ \frac{h_m^{(N)}(n)}{\mu_m^{(N)}}
      \ =\ \frac{(n)_m}{(N)_m\mu_m^{(N)}}
        \ =\ \frac{\binom{n}{m}}{\binom{N}{m}\mu_m^{(N)}},
      \qquad n,m\in\{0,\ldots,N\},
   \end{equation}
   where $Y^{(N)}$ is a death process with state space $\{0,\ldots,N\}$, whose generator $L^{Y^{(N)}}$ acts on functions $f:\{0,\ldots,N\}\to\rz$ via
   \begin{equation} 
      L^{Y^{(N)}}f(m)\ =\ \frac{1}{\mu_m^{(N)}}\sum_{k=1}^m \varphi(k,m)\mu_{m-k}^{(N)}\big(f(m-k)-f(m)\big),
      \qquad m\in\{0,\ldots,N\}.
   \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 10 of arXiv:2511.06196, 'Central limit theorem for high temperature spin models via martingale embedding'] Let \(A \subset \{1,\ldots,n\}\) and choose arbitrary \(\epsilon > 0\).
Then, for some \(C_{t,\epsilon}, c_\epsilon > 0\), independent of \(n\) and \(A\), we have
\begin{equation*}
\sup_{\sigma \in \{-1,1\}^{A}}
\E\Big|\sum_{j=1}^nm_{ij}^{[A]}\Big|^{2+\epsilon}
\le C_{t,\epsilon}
\end{equation*}
for all \(i\in \{1,\ldots,n\}\) and \(0 \le t < c_\epsilon\).
Hereafter, $\sup_{\sigma \in \{-1,1\}^{A}}$ is over all configurations of the spins in $A$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma homo of arXiv:2411.04157, 'Stochastic homogenization of dynamical discrete optimal transport'] [{Copy of $\mathbb{Z}^n$ in $(\mathcal X,\mathcal E)$}]
Assume (equation path of the paper) and (equation ball of the paper) in Assumption (ass: graph of the paper). Then there is a graph homomorphism $\phi$ from a subdivision of $ (\mathbb{Z}^n,n.n.)$ to $(\mathcal X,\mathcal E)$, i.e.\ a map $\phi: \mathbb{Z}^n\to\mathcal X$ and a map also called $\phi: n.n.\to \{\text{paths in }(\mathcal X,\mathcal E)\}$ such that $\phi((z,z'))$ is a path from $\phi(z)$ to $\phi(z')$ , with  
\begin{align*}
    &|\phi(z)-z|\leq R, \quad\forall z\in\mathbb{Z}^n\\
    &L(\phi(z,z'))\leq 2R(R+1) \quad \forall z,z'\ n.n. \text{ in } \mathbb{Z}^n.
\end{align*}

[Definition 3 (Gromov-Wasserstein Distance, \cite{memoli2011gromov}) of arXiv:2608.27500, 'Optimal Transport for Network Comparison: \\ A Review with Machine Learning Applications'] For $1\leq p<\infty$, the Gromov-Wasserstein distance between $(V,d_V,\mu_G)$ and $(V',d_{V'},\mu_{G'})$ is
\begin{align}
    GW_p(G,G')=\bigg(\min_{\pi\in \Pi(\mu_G,\mu_{G'})}\sum_{i,j=1}^n\sum_{k,l=1}^m |d_V(i,j)-d_{V'}(k,l)|^p\pi_{ik}\pi_{jl}\bigg)^\frac{1}{p}.
\end{align}

[Lem 44 of arXiv:2510.23423, 'One-arm exponents of the high-dimensional Ising model'] Let $G=(V,E)$ be a finite graph and $\beta\geq 0$. Let $x,y\in V$ and $\gamma:x\rightarrow y$ such that $\rho_{G,\beta}(\gamma)>0$. Then, if $\mathcal E$ is measurable in terms of $\n_{|\overline{\gamma}^c}$,
\begin{equation}
	Z^{xy}_{G,\beta}[\Gamma(\n)=\gamma, \mathcal E]=Z^{xy}_{G,\beta}[\Gamma(\n)=\gamma] \mathbf P^\emptyset_{\overline{\gamma}^c,\beta}[\mathcal E].
\end{equation}

