[Proposition 8 (Exponential decay of hitting times) of arXiv:2607.09575, 'Probabilistic estimates for a system of\\noisy integrate-and-fire neurons'] For any $i,k \ge 1$ and for all $p > 1$, sufficiently close to $1$, we have
	\begin{equation*}
		\prob\left[\tau_k^i \le T\right] \le C^{\frac{1}{p}}e^{-\frac{(k-1)^2\gamma^2(p-1)}{2pTC_\sigma^2}},
	\end{equation*}
	for some constant $C > 0$ that depends on $p$ and $T$ but holds uniformly in $i,\,k$, and $N$, and $ \gamma$ is the constant in \cref{ass: ASSUMPTIONS FROM SOJMARK SPDE PAPER APPLIED TO THE NEUROSCIENCE SETTING} \eqref{ass: ASSUMPTIONS FROM SOJMARK SPDE PAPER APPLIED TO THE NEUROSCIENCE SETTING FOUR}.

