[Definition 13 ({\cite[Definition 6.1) of arXiv:2511.13404, 'Asymptotic stability and mean ergodicity of Feller processes on Polish spaces'] {Villani2009}}]
		Let $d$ be a semicontinuous metric on $(\X,\rho)$. For $p\in[1,\infty)$ \emph{$p$-Wasserstein distance for $d$} on $\mathcal{P}(\X)$ is
		\begin{equation*}
			W_{p,d}(\mu,\nu) := \inf_{\pi \in \mathscr{C}{(\mu,\nu)}}\left\{\int_{\X\times \X}d(x,y)^p\pi(\d x,\d y)\right\}^{1/p}.
		\end{equation*}

[Prop of arXiv:2507.09421, 'Stability of randomly switching stochastic reaction networks with asymptotically linear tr'] In Example (ex:IDK man of the paper), if Conjecture (conj: general conjecture of the paper) holds then for each fixed value of $\alpha>0$, for all small enough values of $\kappa$ every state is evanescent for $Z_\kappa$.
\begin{proof}
    The matrices associated to $\mathcal R_1$ and $\mathcal R_2$ are given by
    \begin{align*}
        M_1=\begin{pmatrix}3&1&0\\1&-1&0\\0&0&-\alpha\end{pmatrix}
        \qquad
        M_2=\begin{pmatrix}-\alpha&0&0\\0&-1&1\\0&1&3\end{pmatrix},
    \end{align*}
    respectively. If $v^1:=(2,1,0)$ and $v^2:=(0,1,2)$, then one has $v^1M_1=(7,1,0)$ and $v^2M_2=(0,1,7)$. Therefore, it would follow from Conjecture (conj: general conjecture of the paper) that for all small enough values of $\kappa$, all states with at least one molecule of $S_2$ were evanescent. But the existence of an inflow reaction for $S_1$ as well as a reaction taking $S_1$ as input and producing $S_2$ means that from every state, it is possible to reach another state with at least one $S_2$. Thus given Conjecture (conj: general conjecture of the paper) every state would be evanescent for small $\kappa$, as claimed.
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 8 (Empirical-measure map) of arXiv:2608.28827, 'Quantitative Target Convergence and Uniform-in-Time\\ Propagation of Chaos for Langevin-Re'] For $X,Y\in(\R^d)^N$,
\begin{equation}
    W_2^2(\mu_X^N,\mu_Y^N)
    \le\frac1N\sum_{i=1}^N\|x_i-y_i\|^2.
\end{equation}

[Lemma box-product-bifun-general of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] For any compact Lie groups $G,H$, the box product defines a homotopical left Quillen bifunctor $\cat{$\bm G$-$\textbf{\textup{L}}$-Top}\times\cat{$\bm H$-$\textbf{\textup{L}}$-Top}\to\cat{$\bm{(G\times H)}$-$\textbf{\textup{L}}$-Top}$.
    \begin{proof}
        The pushout-product axiom for cofibrations is verified in [barrero2021]*{Lemma A.6}, while the preservation of weak equivalences is Corollary 3.10 of \emph{op.\ cit.}

        For the pushout product axiom for acyclic cofibrations, we will more generally show that the pushout product of any h-cofibration $i$ (of orthogonal $H$-spaces) with any $G$-global weak equivalence $f$ is a $(G\times H)$-global weak equivalence. As both h-cofibrations and weak equivalences are stable under restriction (see Lemma~(lemma:restr-right-Quillen of the paper)), this follows at once by applying [barrero2021]*{Corollary~A.10} to ${\textup{triv}}_Gi$ and ${\textup{triv}}_Hf$.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

