[Definition 5 of arXiv:2604.11682, 'Semilocalization for inhomogeneous random graphs'] We define the strict order relation $\prec$ on the set of vertices $[N]$ as
  follows. For any two vertices $x, y \in [N]$,
  \begin{equation*}
    x \prec y \text{ if and only if } \Bigl( (D_{x} < D_{y}) \text{ or } (D_{x} = D_{y} \text{ and } x > y) \Bigr)\,.
  \end{equation*}

  We define the (random) permutation $\pi \in \Sym_{N}$ to be the
  unique permutation such that
  \begin{equation*}
    \pi(N) \prec \pi(N-1) \prec \cdots \prec \pi(2) \prec \pi(1)\,.
  \end{equation*}

[Theorem Lebesgue of arXiv:2608.10415, 'Continuous random variables'] Assuming $f_n\to f$ pointwise, and assuming that we have $|f_n|\leq g$, for a certain function $g$ satisfying $\int g<\infty$, the following happen:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\int|f|<\infty$.

\item $\int|f_n-f|\to0$.

\item $\int f_n\to\int f$.
\end{enumerate}

