[Lemma 7 (Distributional recursion for \(\Svar^{(n)}\)) of arXiv:2604.13149, 'The Distributional Tail of Worst--Case Quickselect'] For every \(n\ge 0\),
\begin{equation}
\Svar^{(n+1)}
\eqd
1+\max\{\U\,(\Svar^{(n)})',\,(1-\U)\,(\Svar^{(n)})''\},
\end{equation}
where \(\U\sim\Unif(0,1)\), the random variables \((\Svar^{(n)})'\) and \((\Svar^{(n)})''\) are
independent copies of \(\Svar^{(n)}\), and \(\U\) is independent of both of them.

[Theorem mainresult:lowrank of arXiv:2608.23765, '\texorpdfstring{Tight Majorizations and Convergence Rates\\ of Nuclear Norm Minimization I'] Let \\ \allowbreak $\mathbf{X}_\star \in  \mathbb{R}^{d_1 \times d_2}$  be a matrix of rank $r$.
Assume that the measurement operator $\mathcal{A}: \mathbb{R}^{d_1 \times d_2} \longrightarrow \R^m $ satisfies the NSP (eq:NSP:definition of the paper) of order $r$ with constant $\eta_r <3/5$. Let $\left(  \mathbf{X}^{(k)}\right)_{k\geq 0}$ and $\left(\varepsilon_{k} \right)_{k \geq 0}$ be the \texttt{MatrixIRLS} iterates obtained from (alg:algo1 of the paper) with measurements $\mathbf{y} = \mathcal{A}(\mathbf{X}_\star)$, rank estimate $\widetilde{r} = r$ and initializing weight operator $W^{(0)}$, and assume that the weight operators $\W{\mathbf{X}^{(k)}}{\varepsilon_{k}}$ are admissible in the sense of (def:admissible:weightoperators of the paper), with associated constant $c_q$ of (eq:c_q:def of the paper).

Then it holds for all iterations $k \in \mathbb{N}_0$ carried out by (alg:algo1 of the paper) that
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{J}_{\varepsilon_{k}} \left( \mathbf{X}^{(k)} \right)  -  \nucnorm{ \mathbf{X}_\star } 
\le 
\left(  1-  \frac{C_{\eta_r}}{c_q\eta_1 d}   \right)^k 
\left(    \mathcal{J}_{\varepsilon_{0}} \left( \mathbf{X}^{(0)} \right) -  \nucnorm{\mathbf{X}_\star} \right)
\end{equation}
as well as
\begin{equation}
\nucnorm{ \mathbf{X}^{(k)} - \mathbf{X}_\star} \le 
A_{\eta_r} \left(  1-  \frac{C_{\eta_r}}{c_q\eta_1 d}   \right)^k  \nucnorm{ \mathbf{X}^{(0)}-\mathbf{X}_\star}
\end{equation}
where $A_{\eta_r}$ and $C_{\eta_r}$ are as in (eq:A_eta_r:def of the paper) and $ 0 < \eta_1 \leq \eta_r$ denotes the NSP constant of $\mathcal{A}$ of order~$1$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition prop_1edge of arXiv:2608.07236, 'Negative association of Busemann functions \\in exponential last-passage percolation'] For any coordinate-wise monotone functions $f$ and $g$ that are monotone in the same direction, it holds that 
\begin{equation}
\textup{$\mathbb{C}$ov}\Big(f\big(B^{\rho_k}(\mathcal{B})\big), g\big( (B^{\rho_j}(\mathcal{R}^j_i))_{i,j=1}^{2k} ,(B^{\rho_j}(\mathcal{G}^j_i))_{i,j=1}^{2k}\big) \Big) \leq 0.
\end{equation}
In addition, if we restrict the range of the index $j$, then we have
\begin{equation}
\textup{$\mathbb{C}$ov}\Big(f\big(B^{\rho_k}(\mathcal{B})\big), g\big( (B^{\rho_j}(\mathcal{R}^j_i))_{i=1, \dots, 2k}^{j=1,\dots, k} ,(B^{\rho_j}(\mathcal{G}^j_i))_{i=1 \dots 2k}^{j=k,\dots, 2k}\big) \Big) = 0.
\end{equation}

