[Theorem 7 of arXiv:2606.23383, 'Near-Lipschitz stability of the Kim--Milman flow map'] For $\mu \in \cP_2(\mathbb{R}^d)$ which satisfies \eqref{theta}, and any $\nu \in \cP_2(\mathbb{R}^d)$, it holds that
\begin{align}
\|\mathsf T^{\mu} - \mathsf T^{\nu}\|_{L^2(\gamma)}^2 \leq 2L_0^2(\mu) \, {\mathsf H}(\nu\mmid\mu)\,,
\end{align}
where $$L_0(\mu) \deq \exp\Bigl(\int_0^\infty \theta_t \dd t\Bigr)\, .$$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma l:global_energy_estimate of arXiv:2607.15140, 'On the absence of blow-up in the 3D Navier-Stokes equations with transport noise'] There exists a constant $C>0$ such that, for any $N\geq 1$, any interval $I=(0,T)\subseteq {\mathbb R}_+$ with $T<\infty$,
$$
w_0\in L^0_{{\mathscr F}_{0}}(\Omega;\mathbb{L}^2(\mathbb{T}^d))\qquad \text{ and }\qquad 
F\in L^0_{\mathscr{P}}(\Omega;L^2(I\times \mathbb{T}^d;{\mathbb R}^{d\times d})),
$$
there exists a unique progressively measurable process 
$$
w^N\in C(\overline{I};\mathbb{L}^2(\mathbb{T}^d))\cap L^2(I;\mathbb{H}^{1}(\mathbb{T}^d))\ \text{ a.s. }
$$ 
which solves the oscillating stochastic Stokes system (equation eq:turbulent_Stokes_scaling_uniform_estimates of the paper) in its natural integrated form (cf.\ Definition (def:p_solution of the paper)), and satisfies the following \emph{pathwise} energy inequality: 
\begin{align}

\sup_I\|w^N\|_{L^{2} (\mathbb{T}^d)}
+\|\nabla w^N\|_{L^{2}(I\times \mathbb{T}^d)}
\leq C \|w_0\|_{L^{2}(\mathbb{T}^d)} + C  \|F\|_{L^2(I\times \mathbb{T}^d)}\ \text{ a.s. }
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

