body:
[Theorem eq:F(beta)SSK+CW of arXiv:2510.26960, 'Free energy fluctuations in SK and related spin glass models: \\ A literature survey'] In the three phases, $F_N$ exhibits the following convergence in distribution as $N\to\infty$. 
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
\item Spin glass: If $\beta>1$ and $J<1$, then
 \begin{equation} 
\frac{2N^{2/3}}{\beta-1}(F_N(\beta)-F(\beta))\to TW_1.
 \end{equation} 
\item Paramagnetic: If $\beta<1$ and $\beta<1/J$, then
 \begin{equation} 
N(F_N(\beta)-F(\beta))\to\mathcal{N}(f_1,\alpha_1)
 \end{equation} 
where $\alpha_1=-\frac12\log(1-\beta^2)$ and $f_1=-\frac12(\alpha_1+\log(1-\beta J))$.
\item Ferromagnetic: If $J>1$ and $\beta>1/J$, then
 \begin{equation} 
N^{1/2}(F_N(\beta)-F(\beta))\to\mathcal{N}(0,\alpha_2)
 \end{equation} 
where $\alpha_2=\frac12(1-J^{-2})(4\beta-J^{-1})^2.$
\end{enumerate}
Here the limiting free energy $F(\beta)$ is given by the formula
 \begin{equation}  
F(\beta)=\begin{cases} \beta-\frac12\log\beta-\frac34&\text{for spin glass,}\\
\frac14\beta^2&\text{for paramagnetic,}\\
\frac\beta2(J+\frac1J)-\frac12\log(\beta J)-\frac{1}{4J^2}-\frac12&\text{for ferromagnetic.}
\end{cases}
 \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Thm 53 (Backwards-time propagation of test functions in $d \geq 3$) of arXiv:2510.22155, 'Random walks in space-time random media in all spatial dimensions: the full subcritical fl'] Let $d\ge 3$. Assume $\x_N=(x^1_N,x^2_N)$ is any sequence such that $N^{-1/2}x_N^1 \to x_1$ and $x^1_N-x^2_N$ converges to $y\in I$ (\textit{without} any normalization), and consider the same setting as Assumption \ref{ass:SRI}. 
      Consider smooth bounded functions $\phi_1, ..., \phi_m : \mathbb R^d\to \mathbb R$ with globally bounded and continuous first and second partial derivatives, and consider $f^1,..., f^m : I\to \mathbb R$ continuous of exponential decay at infinity. Then \begin{align} 
    \lim_{N\to\infty}\notag  \Ebbnn &\bigg[\sum_{0\leq r_1 < \ldots < r_m \leq Nt} \prod_{\ell =1}^m f^{\ell}(   R^1_{r_{\ell}}-   R^2_{r_{\ell}})\phi_{\ell}(N^{-1/2} (R^1_{r_\ell}-   s_N r_\ell))\bigg] \\&=  \phi_1 (x_1) \cdots \phi_m(x_1) \sum_{0\le r_1 < ...< r_m<\infty } \; \mathbf E^{\boldsymbol b_{\mathrm{dif}}}_y \big[ f^1(X_{r_1}) \cdots f^m(X_{r_m}) ].\end{align}
    The claim remains true if we replace $(f^1,...,f^m)$ on the left side by some sequence $(f^1_N,...,f^m_N)$ equicontinuous and converging uniformly on compact sets as $N\to\infty$ to some $(f^1,...,f^m)$, as long as $\sup_{N} |f^j_N|$ decays exponentially fast at infinity for $j=1,...,m$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

