[Thm tree-lower of arXiv:2609.03856, 'Consensus time for asynchronous $\ell^p$ relaxation: graph dependence'] For every $1<p<\infty$, there is $f_0\in[0,1]^V$ of range exactly one such
that
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb{E}\tau_p(1/2;f_0)\ge c_p\frac{nT_G}{\Delta}.
\end{equation}

[Lemma lem:properce of arXiv:2505.12187, 'Speeding up quantum Markov processes through lifting'] Let $E_{\mathcal{N}} : \mathcal{M} \to \mathcal{N}$ a conditional expectation with respect to $\sigma$. Then $E_{\mathcal{N}}$ is self-adjoint with respect to the $s$-inner product $\langle \cdot ,\cdot \rangle_{\sigma,s}$ for any $s \in [0,1]$, where the inner product $\langle \cdot ,\cdot \rangle_{\sigma,s}$ is defined as in (equation def:s-inner of the paper). It follows that $E_{\mathcal{N}}$ commutes with the modular automorphism group:
\begin{align*}
    \Delta_\sigma^{is} \circ E_{\mathcal{N}} = E_{\mathcal{N}} \circ  \Delta_\sigma^{is}\,,\quad \forall s \in \mathbb{R}\,,
\end{align*}
where $\Delta_\sigma$ is the modular operator in (equation def:modularoperator of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Def of arXiv:2601.10568, 'Hydrodynamic Limit with a Weierstrass-type result'] In what follows, let $\eta\in\Omega_N$ , $f:\Omega_N\to\mathbb{R}$ and $x,y,z\in\mathbb{T}_N$ be arbitrary:
\begin{itemize}
        \item Let ${\textcolor{blue}{\pi_x}}:\Omega_N\to\{0,1\}$ be the projection $\pi_x(\eta)=\eta(x)$;

        \item The shift operator, ${\textcolor{blue}{\tau}}:\eta\mapsto\tau\eta$, is defined through $\pi_x(\tau\eta)=\pi_{x+1}(\eta)$. We short-write ${\textcolor{blue}{\tau^i}}=\circ_{j=1}^i\tau$ for its $i$-th composition;

        \item Denote by $\ndef{\theta_{x,y}}$ the operator that exchanges the occupation-value of the sites $x,y$,
        \begin{align}
            (\theta_{x,y}\eta)(z)
            =\eta(z)1_{z\neq x,y}
            +\eta(y)1_{z=x}
            +\eta(x)1_{z=y};
        \end{align}

        \item For $\mathcal{O}=\tau,\theta_{x,y}$, let $\ndef{\mathcal{O} f(\eta)}:=f(\mathcal{O}\eta)$.
    \end{itemize}
    Moreover, introduce the linear operators $\ndef{\nabla_{x,y}},{\textcolor{blue}{\nabla}},{\textcolor{blue}{\Delta}}$ through 
    \begin{align}
        \nabla_{x,y}[f]:=\theta_{x,y}f-f
        ,\quad
        \nabla[f]:=\tau f-f 
        \quad\text{and}\quad
        \Delta[f]=\nabla^2[\tau^{-1}f]
        .
    \end{align}
    Note that $\nabla$ corresponds to the forward difference operator and $\Delta$ to the discrete Laplacian operator.

[Proposition 9 of arXiv:2607.07283, 'Multi-time Markov renewal chains and stratified renewal theorems'] Let $G$ be a non-negative function sequence and put $L=\psi*G$. Then $L$ is the minimal non-negative solution of the Markov renewal equation

\begin{equation*}
L=G+q*L.
\end{equation*}

Equivalently,

\begin{equation*}
L_k(x)=\Ee_x\left[\sum_{n\geq0}\one_{\{S_n\leqd k\}}G_{k-S_n}(J_n)\right].
\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

