{"artifact_digest":"sha256:870d58d966ecaaff58c16ec010f92cd87df2f995f5eea2be22557eb5f69ca013","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/87/870d58d966ecaaff58c16ec010f92cd87df2f995f5eea2be22557eb5f69ca013.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:ce3efa0488f9d746de94d3c657310fac3b1f31e84a2690ca9b8062c2d2660d3c","bundle_entry_key":"2606-09121-lemma-7-pathwise-122","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_046","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_046.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2606-09121-lemma-7-pathwise-122","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: It suffices to verify the formulas for smooth paths and then pass to the geometric rough-path limit, since the displayed signature coordinates are continuous and the right-hand sides are continuous under uniform convergence.  Write $S(z)_{a,b}$ for the signature of the subpath on $[a,b]$.  Since the first coordinate of $z$ is the bounded-variation path $u\mapsto u$, the recursive definition of iterated integrals gives
\begin{equation}
    \pi_{122}S(z)=\int_0^1 \pi_{22}S(z)_{u,1}\,\dd u,
    \qquad
    \pi_{221}S(z)=\int_0^1 \pi_{22}S(z)_{0,u}\,\dd u.
\end{equation}
