body:
[Definition def:rho of arXiv:2605.17082, 'Spectral Purification in Reversible Markov Chains: Hidden Parameters, Observable Equivalen'] The \emph{relative dissipation rate} at step $k$ is
\begin{equation}
\rho_k := \frac{E_{k+1}}{E_k} \in (0,1).
\end{equation}
Equivalently, the fraction of energy dissipated in step $k$ is $d_k := 1 - \rho_k$.

[Lemma 6 of arXiv:2605.21424, 'A dice game, a multinomial walk, and the inverted Dirichlet distribution'] Let \(m\ge 2\) be an integer and \(n_1,\ldots,n_m\in (0,\infty)\).
Let $(G_1,\dotsc,G_m)$ be a vector of independent random variables with $G_k\sim\operatorname{Gamma}(n_k,1)$, then
\begin{align*}
\left(\frac{G_2}{G_1},\dotsc,\frac{G_m}{G_1}\right)\sim \operatorname{ID}(n_2,\dotsc,n_m;n_1)
\end{align*}
and 
\begin{align*}
\left(\frac{G_1}{\sum_{k=1}^m G_k},\dotsc,\frac{G_m}{\sum_{k=1}^m G_k}\right)\sim \operatorname{D}(n_1,\dotsc,n_m),
\end{align*}
where \(\operatorname{D}(n_1,\dotsc,n_m)\) denotes the Dirichlet distribution with parameter set \((n_1,\dotsc,n_m)\) (see, e.g., \cite[Example 6.1]{Fang_et_al_1990} for the definition).

In particular, if $(V_1,\dotsc,V_m)$ is Dirichlet distributed with parameter set $(n_1,\dotsc,n_m)$ then, defining 
\begin{align*}
X_k:=\frac{V_k}{1-V_2-\dotsb-V_m},\quad 2\leq k\leq m,
\end{align*}
we get $(X_2,\dotsc,X_m)\sim \operatorname{ID}(n_2,\dotsc,n_m;n_1)$. 

Conversely, if $(X_2,\dotsc,X_m)\sim \operatorname{ID}(n_2,\dotsc,n_m;n_1)$, then 
\begin{align*}
V:=\Big(\frac{1}{1+X_2+\dotsb+X_m},\frac{X_2}{1+X_2+\dotsb+X_m},\dotsc,\frac{X_m}{1+X_2+\dotsb+X_m}\Big)
\end{align*}
 is Dirichlet distributed with parameter set $(n_1,\dotsc,n_m)$.

[Proposition 40 of arXiv:2605.10150, 'Rough path theory and an introduction to rough partial differential equations'] Let $\beta, \gamma \in (0,1]$ with $\beta \leq \gamma$ be arbitrary. Furthermore, let $Y \in \calc^{\beta}([0,T],W)$ and $f \in C_b^{\gamma,1}([0,T] \times W, \bar{W})$ be arbitrary. Then we have $f(Y) \in \calc^{\beta}([0,T],\bar{W})$, where $f(Y) : [0,T] \to \bar{W}$ denotes the path
\begin{align*}
f(Y)_t := f(t,Y_t), \quad t \in [0,T].
\end{align*}

