{"artifact_digest":"sha256:e672d376c2d4bf482e3c55b590ced8af879f2423c2b54f3282ae72993c215648","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/e6/e672d376c2d4bf482e3c55b590ced8af879f2423c2b54f3282ae72993c215648.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:b298445c39ebf2e5473b1b05a03101f873f8b5670094ffdfdbc18de34a038085","bundle_entry_key":"2607-19511-theorem-15-thm-level-3","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s1_045","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s1_045.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2607-19511-theorem-15-thm-level-3","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Suppose first that $c$ is realizable as a $\Pi$-based probability mixture.  That is, there is a finitely additive probability measure $Q$ on $(\Pi, {\cal D}_\Pi)$ with $c_G(E) = Q([E, G]_\Pi)$ for every $E \in \cal F$ and $G \in \cal G$.  Define $\mu$ according to Equation~(eq-assignment of the paper).  This is well-defined since if $[E, G]_\Pi = [F, H]_\Pi$, we have:
\begin{equation}
c_G(E) = Q([E, G]_\Pi) = Q([F, H]_\Pi) = c_H(F),
\end{equation}
