{"artifact_digest":"sha256:a8cd60decf88aac8ac6030063f612f4b6a67361f33651fa443ffa6b2d7a0d509","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/a8/a8cd60decf88aac8ac6030063f612f4b6a67361f33651fa443ffa6b2d7a0d509.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:94bb51daa2010f3139cd4c1c02463bbb73844744bb34c4e37f0edcb864fc9f61","bundle_entry_key":"2601-10624-lemma-28-pd","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_045","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s0_045.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2601-10624-lemma-28-pd","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Let $d \geqslant 5$. Let $(X_n)_{n \geqslant 0}$ and $(Y_n)_{n \geqslant 0}$ be two independent simple random walks in $\mathbb{Z}^d$ started at the origin. Further, let $I$ be the number of intersections of the two random walks, including the starting point, namely,
    \begin{equation*}
        I = \left|\left\{(i,j) \in \mathbb{Z}_{\geqslant 0}^2: X_i = Y_j\right\}\right|.
    \end{equation*}
    Then, [Law2] implies
    \begin{equation}

        s(d) \geqslant \mathbb{E}[I]^{-1}.
