[Theorem t: main theorem displacement of arXiv:2605.22803, 'Persistence of asymptotic variance under transport:\\ from hyperfluctuation to stealthy hy'] Let $p\in[-d,\infty)$. Suppose that $Z$ is an invariant $\R^d$-valued random field on $\R^d$, that $\Phi$ is an invariant locally square-integrable random measure, and that $\Psi$ is defined as in~(equation e: definition Psi displacement of the paper). Either assume that for some $\vartheta>0$ and strongly log-dominating function $\rho$,
    \begin{equation}
        \BE_0^\Phi[\Phi(B_\vartheta) \|Z(0)\|^{d+\max(p,0)} \rho(\|Z(0\|))] <\infty,
    \end{equation}
    or assume that $Z$ and $\Phi$ are independent and that
    \begin{equation}
        \BE[\|Z(0)\|^{d+\max(p,0)}] < \infty.
    \end{equation}
    Further, assume that $Z^q\cdot\Phi$ is (beyond) $(p-2q)$-uniform for $q\in\N_0$ with $q<\max(\frac{d+p}{2},1)$. Then $\Psi$ is (beyond) $p$-uniform.
    Alternatively, let $\varepsilon>0$ and assume that 
    \begin{equation}
        \BE_0^\Phi\big[\Phi(B_\vartheta) e^{2\varepsilon Z(0)}\big] < \infty,
    \end{equation}
    for some $\vartheta>0$, and that $Z^q\cdot\Phi$ is $\infty$-uniform with radius $\varepsilon$ for $q\in\N_0$. Then $\Psi$ also is $\infty$-uniform with radius $\varepsilon$.
    \begin{proof}
        The assertion is a simple specialization of Theorem~(t:main theorem of the paper) to the special case of displacement kernels, where Condition~(c:condition square-integrable general with p of the paper) can be obtained from~(equation e: random field moment condition of the paper) analogous to Proposition~(p:independent square-integrable of the paper).
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Cor cor14 of arXiv:2609.02795, 'Global smooth behavior in Kuznetsov and Westervelt type\\ viscous wave equations:\\ A unif'] Let $p\ge 2$. Then there exist $\Gamma_4=\Gamma_4(p,a,h)>0$ and $\Gamma_5=\Gamma_5(p,a,h)>0$ such that if ((init of the paper)) 
  and ((uut of the paper)) hold with $\delta_1=\delta_1(h)$ as in Lemma (lem11 of the paper) and with some $T\in (0,T_{max}]$, then the function ${\mathcal{F}}_1$
  from ((F1 of the paper)) satisfies
  \begin{eqnarray}\label{14.1}
	& & \hspace*{-20mm}
	\frac{d}{dt} {\mathcal{F}}_1^\frac{p}{2}(t)
	+ \Gamma_4 \cdot {\mathcal{F}}_1^\frac{p}{2}(t)

	\nonumber\\
	&\le& \Gamma_5 \cdot \bigg\{ \int_\Omega |u| \cdot |f(u,u_t,\nabla u, \nabla u_t)| \bigg\}^\frac{p}{2}
	+ \Gamma_5 \cdot \bigg\{ \int_\Omega |v|\cdot |f(u,u_t,\nabla u,\nabla u_t)|\bigg\}^\frac{p}{2} \nonumber\\
	& & + \Gamma_5 \cdot \bigg\{ \int_\Omega |u|^3 \bigg\}^\frac{p}{2}
	+ \Gamma_5 \cdot \bigg\{ \int_\Omega |v|^3 \bigg\}^\frac{p}{2}
	\qquad \mbox{for all } t\in (0,T).
  \end{eqnarray} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma eq_1650 of arXiv:2605.10479, 'Poisson approximation of random lattices'] Let $A\subseteq B\subseteq \mathbb R^n$ be finite sets, and assume that $A$ consists
of linearly independent vectors. Then for every $R_1,R_2\geq 0$ with $R_1$
