[Proposition prop:supermartingale of arXiv:2608.06263, 'Almost sure path localisation for the derivative martingale of branching Brownian motion'] Consider $\mathcal{Z}$ the stopping line associated with the stopping times
    \begin{equation*}
        \zeta^{(u)} \coloneq
        \begin{cases}
            \infty & \text{if $\zeta^{(v)} < \infty$ for some strict ancestor $v$ of $u$}, \\
            \inf\{t \ge 0 : f(X_u(t)) \le 0\} & \text{otherwise}.
        \end{cases}
    \end{equation*}
    Assume that, for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$, the process $(M_t)_{t \ge 0}$ is an $((\mathscr{F}_t)_{t \ge 0},\mathbb{P}_x)$-martingale and that, for any $t \ge 0$, we have $\mathcal{L}_t \preceq \mathcal{Z}$.
    Then, for any $y \in \mathbb{R}$, the stopped process $(M_{\tau_t})_{t \ge 0}$ is a non-negative $((\mathscr{F}_{\mathcal{L}_t})_{t \ge 0},\mathbb{P}_y)$-supermartingale and therefore converges $\mathbb{P}_y$-almost surely as $t \to \infty$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma lem:D_L of arXiv:2412.05491, 'Near-critical and finite-size scaling for \\ high-dimensional lattice trees and animals'] Let $L\ge L_2$ with $L_2$ sufficiently large, let $m \ge 0$, and let
$\alpha$ be any multi-index.
Then
\begin{align}

\bignorm { \nabla^\alpha \hat D ^{(m)} }_{q } &\lesssim L^{\abs\alpha - d/q} (1+mL)e^{mL}	
\qquad ( 0 \le q^{-1} < 1 ).
\end{align}

