[Lemma lem:clt_1 of arXiv:2411.14818, 'Scaling limits of solitons in the Box-Ball system'] Let $\zeta(i)$, $i \in {\mathbb{N}}$ be i.i.d. random variables define on any probability space with ${\mathbb{E}}[\zeta(0)] = 0$ and ${\mathbb{E}}[\zeta(0)^2] = 1$, and define $S(n) := \sum_{i = 1}^{n} \zeta(i)$, $n \in {\mathbb{N}}$. Assume that $a_{\varepsilon}\left(t\right), b_{\varepsilon}\left(t\right)$ are non-deacresing function on $[0,\infty)$ such that {for any ${\mathbf{T}} > 0$,}
                \begin{align}
                    \lim_{\varepsilon \to 0} \sup_{0 \le t \le {\mathbf{T}}}\left|a_{\varepsilon}\left(t\right) -  b_{\varepsilon}\left(t\right)\right| = 0, \quad \lim_{\varepsilon \to 0} \sup_{0 \le t \le {\mathbf{T}}}\left|a_{\varepsilon}\left(t\right) -  a t \right| = 0,
                \end{align}
            with some constant $a > 0$. Then, for any ${\mathbf{T}} > 0$ and $\delta > 0$, we have 
                \begin{align}
                    \lim_{\varepsilon \to 0} \mathbb{P}\left( \sup_{0 \le t \le {\mathbf{T}}} \varepsilon \left| S\left( \left\lfloor \frac{a_{\varepsilon}\left(t\right)}{\varepsilon^2} \right\rfloor \right) - S\left( \left\lfloor \frac{b_{\varepsilon}\left(t\right)}{\varepsilon^2} \right\rfloor \right) \right| > \delta \right) = 0.
                \end{align}

