[Theorem 3 of arXiv:2607.17087, 'Transportation-cost inequalities for invariant measures of stochastic reaction-diffusion e'] Let $\{\sigma(s,y): (s,y)\in\mathbb{R}_+\times [0,1]\}$ be a progressively measurable random field such that the following stochastic integral is well-defined. Then for any $\alpha>0$, $T\geq 0$, and $p>2$,

  \begin{align}
     \sup_{t\in [0,T]} \mathbb{E} \sup_{x\in [0,1]}\left|\int_0^t\int_0^1 e^{-\alpha (t-s)}p_{t-s}(x,y)\sigma(s,y)\,W(ds,dy) \right|^2
    \leq   \frac{K_p}{\alpha^{\frac{p-2}{2p}}} \sup_{s\in [0,T]} \mathbb{E}\sup_{y\in[0,1]}| \sigma(s,y)|^2,

  \end{align}

where the constant $K_p$ is independent of $T$ and defined by
\begin{align}
  K_p:=\min_{\frac{1}{2p} < \theta< \frac{1}{4}} \left\{\left|\frac{\sin\pi\theta}{\pi}\right|^2 (2\pi)^{-\frac1{p}} \left(\frac{p-1}{p}\right)^{\frac{p-1}{p}}  2^{2\theta - \frac{1}{2}} \left( \Gamma\big(\theta - \frac{1}{2p}\big) \right)^2 \times \frac{p-1}{2\sqrt{\pi}} \Gamma\big(\frac{1}{2} - 2\theta\big) \right\},
\end{align}

where $\Gamma(\cdot)$ is the gamma function. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Prop Aut-Vect-delta of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] The space $\textup{Aut}(\ul\textup{Rep}^\amalg_\delta)$ is an Eilenberg--MacLane space of type $K({\mathbb{Z}}/2,1)$. The unique non-trivial automorphism of the identity is given by acting with the generator of $C_2$.
    \begin{proof}
        For the first half, note that in the diagram
        \begin{equation}
            \textup{Aut}(\ul\textup{Rep}^\amalg_\delta)\xrightarrow{\;\ev\;}{\textup{End}}({\mathcal{F}}_{C_2}^\amalg)\xrightarrow{\;\text{res}\;}{\textup{Hom}}(BC_2,{\mathcal{F}}_{C_2})
        \end{equation}
        the first map is a monomorphism (i.e., an inclusion of path components) by Proposition~(prop:Vect-delta-RKE of the paper), while the second one is so by Lemma~(lemma:FC2-free of the paper). We now observe that any automorphism of ${\mathcal{F}}_{C_2}$ has to send the object $C_2$ to itself (as this is the only object with precisely two endomorphisms), so that $((eq:Aut-Vect-delta-vs-Aut-BC2 of the paper))$ factors through $\textup{Aut}(BC_2)$. The latter is connected (and $\textup{Aut}(\ul\textup{Rep}^\amalg_\delta)$ is non-empty), so the inclusion of path components $\textup{Aut}(\ul\textup{Rep}^\amalg_\delta)\to\textup{Aut}(BC_2)$ has to be an equivalence. It is clear that $\textup{Aut}(BC_2)$ is a $K({\mathbb{Z}}/2,1)$, with the unique non-trivial automorphism of the identity of $BC_2$ given by the natural transformation $\tau$ corresponding to the generator of $C_2$, and that $((eq:Aut-Vect-delta-vs-Aut-BC2 of the paper))$ sends the natural transformation given by acting with the generator to $\tau$, which then completes the proof of the proposition. 
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma l.rep_grad_psi_2 of arXiv:2411.14105, 'Simultaneous replica-symmetry breaking \\ for vector spin glasses'] Under~(i.setting of the paper), assume further that the joint decomposition is pinned and fix $X_0 =0$ (hence, $R(0)$ and $A_0$ are deterministic). 
Then, we have
\begin{gather}
    L_p(t) = R(t),\quad\forall t\in\supp \mathrm{d}\alpha.
\end{gather}

