[Propo c_k_star of arXiv:2606.15339, 'Uniform integrability of the distance to the nearest leaf in random trees'] Let $(\mathcal{T}_n)_{n\geq1}$ be a sequence of size-conditioned simply generated trees and set $c_k^{*}:=\mathbf{P}(\partial(\mathcal{T}^{*})\geq k)$. Then $c_0^{*}=1$, and in types~I and~II, for every $k\geq1$,
	\begin{align}
		c_k^{*}=F_k(1)\prod_{i=1}^{k-2}G'(F_i(1))=F_k(1)\,c_{k-1}\,,
	\end{align}
	with the conventions that the empty product equals $1$ and $c_0=1$; in particular $c_1^{*}=1-p_0$ and $c_2^{*}=F_2(1)$. This admits the closed form
	\begin{align}
		c_k^{*}=\frac{F_k(1)\,F_{k-1}'(1)}{m}\,,\qquad k\geq2\,,
	\end{align}
	so that $c_k^{*}=F_k(1)\,F_{k-1}'(1)$ for $k\geq2$ when $m=1$ (type~I). In type~III the marked vertex of $\mathcal{T}^{*}$ is almost surely a leaf, so $\partial(\mathcal{T}^{*})=0$ almost surely, and hence $c_0^{*}=1$ and $c_k^{*}=0$ for every $k\geq1$.

