[Lem DistribXrho of arXiv:2411.14766, 'Axis-Driven Random Walks on $\Z^2$ \\ (transient cases)'] Assume $0<\alpha<1$, there exists $ {\mathbf{c_{_0}}}>0$ such that for $x$ large if $\overline{y}>x$. There exists a function ${\bf c} : \mathbb{N}^2 \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$, such that for any  $\overline{y}-x \leq A x^{\alpha} $, 
\begin{align} 
    \mathbb{P}_x({X}_{\rho}=y) \sim \frac{{\bf c}(x,y)}{2 \overline{y}^{\alpha}}e^{\frac{3}{4}(\overline{y}^{1-\alpha}-x^{1-\alpha}) }, 
\end{align}
moreover ${\bf c}$ depends only on $(\overline{y}-x)/x^{\alpha}$ and if $\overline{y}^{\alpha}-x^{\alpha}=o(x^{\alpha})$ then ${\bf c}(x,y)\sim 1$.
Otherwise
 \begin{align} 
    \mathbb{P}_x({X}_{\rho}=y) \leq  {\bf c}' \prod_{z=x}^{\overline{y}} \frac{1}{\overline{z}^{\alpha}}. 
    \end{align}

[Theorem 31 (Wiener chaos decomposition) of arXiv:2605.14630, '2605.14630'] The Hilbert space $\cH$ can be decomposed into the infinite orthogonal sum 
\begin{equation}
 \cH = \bigoplus_{n=0}^\infty \cH_n\;.
\end{equation}

