[Proposition c:cltspindisag of arXiv:2605.28430, 'Limit theory for Lipschitz-localized statistics in random geometric models'] Let \(\mathcal{P}\) be a simple, stationary point process on \(\mathbb{R}^d\) with summable exponential mixing correlations as in Definition (def.A2 of the paper), and assume that the (reduced) Palm intensity function of the ground process \(\mathcal{P}\) is bounded at all orders, as specified in (equation bdedreduced of the paper). Let \(\Psi = \Psi(\cdot)\) be a stabilizing interaction graph on finite windows with respect to \(\mathcal{P}\) as in Definition~(d:stabilizing-graph of the paper), with finite averaged connective constant \(\bar\Delta<\infty\)  defined in Definition~(d:connective-constant-pp of the paper). If, for some $\epsilon>0$,  all sets \( B \in\mathcal{B}_b(r_0,n_0) \)
defined in~(equation e.particular-sets of the paper), and almost every realization \(\mathcal{P} \), the maximum influence \( q(\Psi(\mathcal{P} \cap B)) \) as in (equation defq of the paper) of the Gibbs specification~(equation eqn:gibbs of the paper) on $\Psi(\mathcal{P}\cap B)$ with finite spin space $\mathbb{K}$ satisfies the inequality 
\begin{equation} 
q(\Psi(\mathcal{P} \cap B)) \le (\epsilon+\bar{\Delta})^{-1}
\end{equation}
 then the spin configuration \( \{V_x(\Psi(\mathcal{P}_n))\}_{x \in \mathcal{P}_n} \) satisfies the CLT as stated in Theorem~(spinmain of the paper), provided the variance condition there is also met. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Thm t:local-holonomies of arXiv:2607.24640, 'The Yang-Mills measure on surfaces via Morse theory'] Let $\gamma$ be a primitive curve with no type $\operatorname{III}$ components and such that the area functional $t\mapsto \int_{\Sigma}[\blacktriangle(\gamma([0,t]))]$ is increasing on $[0,1]$ (including the case of \S(ss:blowup-Wiener-max of the paper) where $\gamma$ is formally reduced to $\{a_{2g+2}\}$). Then, the following holds:
\begin{enumerate}
 \item there exists a unique solution $\mathrm{g}_\gamma$ to~(equation e:SDE-holonomy-Ito of the paper) which verifies $\mathbb{E}\left(\int_0^1\|\mathrm{g}_\gamma(t)\|^2\mathscr{A}_\gamma'(t)dt\right)<\infty,$ where $\|.\|$ is a norm on $\operatorname{M}_N( \mathbb{C} )$;
 \item the map $t\in[0,1]\mapsto \mathrm{g}_\gamma(t)$ is almost surely continuous and $\mathrm{g}_\gamma(t)$ is $\mathcal{F}_\gamma(t)$-measurable for every $t\in[0,1]$;
 \item almost surely, $\mathrm{g}_\gamma(t)\in G$ for all $t\in[0,1]$;
 \item the distribution of $\mathrm{g}_\gamma(t)$ is given by $p_{\mathscr{A}_\gamma(t)}(\mathrm{h})\mu_G(d\mathrm{h})$;
 \item for every $p\geqslant 1$ and for every $0\leqslant t_1< t_2<\ldots< t_p\leqslant 1$, $\mathrm{g}_{\gamma}(t_1)$, $\mathrm{g}_{\gamma}(t_1)^{-1}\mathrm{g}_{\gamma}(t_2)$, $\ldots$, $\mathrm{g}_{\gamma}(t_{p-1})^{-1}\mathrm{g}_{\gamma}(t_p)$ are independent;
 \item for all $0\leq s\leq t\leq 1$, $\mathrm{g}_{\gamma}(s)^{-1}\mathrm{g}_{\gamma}(t)$ has the same distribution as $\mathrm{g}_{\gamma}(t-s)$;
 \item for every $\mathrm{h}$ in $G$, $\mathrm{g}_{\gamma}(t)$ and $\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}_{\gamma}(t)\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ have the same distribution.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

