{"artifact_digest":"sha256:1cfcc9cb1ec86763840159e54ca7537daca573e02104d4e044e3ba8e302d5b63","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/1c/1cfcc9cb1ec86763840159e54ca7537daca573e02104d4e044e3ba8e302d5b63.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:07802fd3834f765d4471d74162a2bde15ce1c0d54f0537be4420c86008fdb483","bundle_entry_key":"2603-28226-theorem-10-weak11","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_003","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_003.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2603-28226-theorem-10-weak11","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: By homogeneity, it is enough to assume $\|a\|_{\ell^\infty}=1$. Replacing $f$ by $\mathbb E[f\mid\mathcal F_\infty]$ does not change the martingale differences and does not increase the $L^1$ norm, so we may assume $f\in L^1(\mathcal F_\infty)$.

Apply Corollary~\ref{cor:stein-gundy} to $f$ at level $\lambda/2$, obtaining
\[
 f=g+h+k.
\]
Then
\begin{equation}

\begin{aligned}
\mathbb P\bigl(|T_N(a;f)|>\lambda\bigr)
&\le \mathbb P\bigl(|T_N(a;g)|>0\bigr)\\
&\quad +\mathbb P\bigl(|T_N(a;h)|>\lambda/2\bigr)
 +\mathbb P\bigl(|T_N(a;k)|>\lambda/2\bigr).
