[Theo 4 of arXiv:2609.00186, 'Macroscopic scalar curvature bounds on surfaces'] Let $(\Sigma,g)$ be a closed oriented Riemannian surface of genus $k\geq2$, normalized such that $\area_g(\Sigma) = \area_{g_0}(\Sigma)$ where $g_0$ is a metric of constant curvature $-1$. Then, for every $r>0$,
\begin{equation}
    \max_{\tilde x\in \tilde \Sigma}\area_{\tilde g}(B_{\tilde g}(\tilde x,r))\geq 2\pi(\cosh{r}-1), 
\end{equation}
with equality for a given $r>0$ if and only if $g$ has constant curvature $-1$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem thm:Polya-Schur full version of arXiv:2605.31356, 'P\'olya--Schur problems and free probability'] Let $\{T_{n}\}$ be a series of operators which commute with $z\partial_z$  such that $\Phi_{T_n}\in\mathcal{LP}$ as defined in (equation eq:Phi_T def of the paper). If $\{p_n\}_{n\geq 1}$ is a sequence of positively-rooted polynomials indexed by their degree, with uniformly bounded roots such that $\llbracket p_n\rrbracket\Rightarrow\mu$ as $n\rightarrow\infty$, and $\{\Phi_{T_n}\}$ satisfy Assumption (assump:main assumption of the paper), then \begin{equation}
		\left\llbracket T_{n}p_n \right\rrbracket\Rightarrow \mu\boxtimes \mathcal{D}_{-1}\mu_{c,\Sigma}^{-1},
	\end{equation} where $\mu_{c,\Sigma}$ is the $\boxplus$-ID limit of the Appell polynomials $A_{n,\Phi_{T_n}}$ from Theorem (thm:main result, general of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

