body:
[Lemma 40 of arXiv:2604.00630, 'The contact process on a bipartite spatial network'] For any~$(\gamma_1,\gamma_2) \in \mathcal S$ and~$a > 0$, we have
	\[1+a-\Delta_2 \nu_\star+\bar{\gamma}_1 \mu_\star\ge \min\left\{\mu_\star,\;1+\bar{\gamma}_2 \nu_\star \right\}.\]
	Consequently,
	\begin{equation*}
		A_\star = \min\left\{\mu_\star,\; 1+\bar{\gamma}_2 \nu_\star \right\}.
	\end{equation*}

[Lemma lemma_modification_of_shift of arXiv:2504.00512, 'Localization-delocalization transition for a random block matrix model at the edge'] For any $1\le k\le DN/2$, suppose that $\left\lVert A \right\rVert_{\text{\rm{HS}}}$ satisfies the condition (equation eq:condA2 of the paper). Defined $z_1$ and $\Delta_{\text{\rm{ev}}}$ as in (equation eq:z1 of the paper) and (equation definition_shift_eigenvector of the paper) for a constant $\varepsilon\in\left({0,1}\right)$.

Then, we have 
\begin{equation}
            \begin{aligned}
                \Delta_{\text{\rm{ev}}}(z_1)=\gamma_k-\gamma_{k}^{\text{\rm{sc}}}+\mathrm{O}\pa{\left\langle \Lambda^2 \right\rangle^2+\left\langle \Lambda^2 \right\rangle\sqrt{\kappa(\gamma_k)+\eta}}=\gamma_k-\gamma_{k}^{\text{\rm{sc}}}+\mathrm{O}\bigg(\frac{\left\lVert A \right\rVert_{\text{\rm{HS}}}^4}{N^{2}}+\frac{k^{ 1/ 3}\left\lVert A \right\rVert_{\text{\rm{HS}}}^2}{N^{4/ 3-{\varepsilon}/{2}}}\bigg).
            \end{aligned}
        \end{equation}
where $\kappa(\gamma_k)=|E^+-\gamma_k|\wedge|\gamma_k-E^-|\sim {k^{2/3}}/{N^{2/3}}$ by (equation eq:kappa_gamma_k of the paper).
The shift $\Delta_{\text{\rm{e}}}$ in (equation definition_shift_eigenvalue of the paper) satisfies a similar bound:         \begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\Delta_{\text{\rm{e}}}(k,\eta)=\gamma_k-\gamma_{k}^{\text{\rm{sc}}}+\mathrm{O}\pa{\left\langle \Lambda^2 \right\rangle^2+\left\langle \Lambda^2 \right\rangle\sqrt{\kappa(\gamma_k)+\eta}}=\gamma_k-\gamma_{k}^{\text{\rm{sc}}}+\mathrm{O}\bigg(\frac{\left\lVert A \right\rVert_{\text{\rm{HS}}}^4}{N^{2}}+\frac{k^{ 1/ 3}\left\lVert A \right\rVert_{\text{\rm{HS}}}^2}{N^{4/ 3-{\varepsilon}/{2}}}\bigg). 
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}













        In particular, if we take $\varepsilon<\varepsilon_A$, the errors in these two estimates are bounded by $N^{-2/3-{\varepsilon}_A}k^{-1/3}$. 

        The corresponding results also hold for $DN/2<k\le DN$.

