[Lemma untied-one-step-estimates of arXiv:2609.03858, 'High-Dimensional Learning Dynamics of \\ Attention-Indexed Models'] Under the conditions of Theorem (theo:untied-SGD of the paper), let \(J\) be a
stopping time satisfying (equation eq:stopping-time of the paper). Set $g^{(n)}:=\nabla_{m_{\mathcal K}}\Phi\left(m^{(n)},q^{(1,n)},q^{(2,n)}\right)$ and $D^{(n)}:=\operatorname{diag}\left(t_k^{A,(n)}+t_k^{B,(n)}
\right)_{k\in\mathcal K}$, and $\vartheta^{(n)}:=\left(
t^{A,(n)},t^{B,(n)},q^{(1,n)},q^{(2,n)}\right)$.
Then, uniformly on \(\{n<J\}\), 
\begin{equation}
\mathbb E_n\left[\Delta m_{\mathcal K}^{(n)}\right]=
-\alpha_dD^{(n)}\left(g^{(n)}+\frac{e^{(n)}}{\sqrt d}\right)+r_m^{(n)},

\end{equation}
where $\|e^{(n)}\|\le C$ and $\|r_m^{(n)}\|\le C\left(\frac{(1+\gamma)\alpha_d}{d}+\alpha_d^2\right)$. Moreover,
\begin{equation}
\mathbb E_n\left[\left\|\Delta m_{\mathcal K}^{(n)}-\mathbb E_n[\Delta m_{\mathcal K}^{(n)}]\right\|^2\right]\le C\alpha_d^2,

\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\left\|\mathbb E_n[\Delta\vartheta^{(n)}]\right\|\le
C\left(\frac{\alpha_d}{\sqrt d}\|g^{(n)}\|+\frac{(1+\gamma)\alpha_d}{d}\right),

\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\mathbb E_n\left[\|\Delta\vartheta^{(n)}\|^2\right]\le
C(1+\gamma)^2\frac{\alpha_d^2}{d}.

\end{equation}
Here $C=C(C_*,\mathfrak D,\mathcal C,\mathcal L,K,L)<\infty$ is independent of \(d,n,T\), and \(\gamma\).

[Proposition 21 (Random-bridge absorption probabilities) of arXiv:2608.16752, 'Absorption Probabilities for Random Convex Hulls: Distribution-Freeness via the Wall-Cross'] Let $V_3,V_4,\dots$ be independent random variables with $V_m\sim\operatorname{Bern}(\frac{1}{m})$, $m\geq 3$.
Then, for $n\geq 2$ and $d\geq 0$,
\begin{equation}
    \pbridge{n}{d}
    =\pr\left(\sum_{m=3}^{n}V_m\geq d\right).

\end{equation}
Consequently, for $n\geq 3$ and $d\geq 1$,
\begin{equation}
    \pbridge{n}{d}
    =\frac{n-1}{n}\pbridge{n-1}{d}
    +\frac{1}{n}\pbridge{n-1}{d-1}.

\end{equation}
Together with the boundary conditions  $\pbridge{n}{0}=1$  for all $n\geq 2$ and  $\pbridge{2}{d}=0$  for all $d\geq 1$, this
recurrence uniquely determines the array.

[Lemma orangoutan of arXiv:2412.02486, 'On the curvatures of \\ random complex submanifolds'] Under the hypotheses of Theorem~(theorem1 of the paper), let $s\in \mathscr{C}^\infty(M,E\otimes L^d)$.  Let $x\in M$ and $f=(f_i)_{i\in \{1, \cdots, 2r\}}$ be the trivialization of $s$ in the setting of Section~(bergman of the paper). 
	Then,
	\begin{equation*}
	 \nabla f(x)= \nabla_0 f(x)= Df(x) \text{ and }\nabla^2 f_d(x) = \frac{1}{\sqrt d^n}
	\left(\nabla_0^2 + \frac{1}dR_0^E \right) f,
		\end{equation*}
where $\nabla_0$ is defined by~((nabla of the paper)).

