[Theorem 7 (Finite-prefix certification theorem) of arXiv:2608.21496, '2608.21496'] For integers $N>m\ge1$, the worst-case bounded-reliability identified width in Eq.~\ref{eq:worstwidthdefinition} is exactly
\begin{equation}
W_{m,N}=B_{m,N}.

\end{equation}
Define two complete joint candidate laws $P_\pm$ by
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
U_\pm&\sim\mathrm{Unif}(0,1),\\
T_\pm\mid U_\pm=u&\sim\mathrm{Bernoulli}\{f_\pm(u)\},\\
f_\pm(u)&=\frac{1\pm\sigma_m(u)}2,
\end{aligned}

\end{equation}
and take candidate sequences iid under each law. These worlds satisfy $\theta_n^+=\theta_n^-=1/2$ for every $n\le m$, but
\begin{equation}
\theta_N^\pm=\frac{1\pm B_{m,N}}2.

\end{equation}
At the common evidence $(\theta_1,\ldots,\theta_m)=(1/2,\ldots,1/2)$, the identified set is exactly
\begin{equation}
\left[\frac{1-B_{m,N}}2,\frac{1+B_{m,N}}2\right];

\end{equation}
every interior value is attained by the iid candidate law with $U\sim\mathrm{Unif}(0,1)$ and $T\mid U=u\sim\mathrm{Bernoulli}\{\lambda f_+(u)+(1-\lambda)f_-(u)\}$ for some $\lambda\in[0,1]$. Consequently Eq.~\ref{eq:exactfrontier} is the full worst-case identified width, not only a lower bound. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition 6 (Weak solution) of arXiv:2609.02022, 'Well-Posedness for SDEs with Logarithmical Critical Distributional Drifts'] Let $\mathfrak F:=(\Omega,\sF,(\sF_t)_{t\geq0},\bP)$ be a stochastic basis, and let $(X,W)$ be a pair of $\mR^d$-valued continuous $\sF_t$-adapted processes on $\mathfrak F$. We call $(\mathfrak F,X,W)$ a weak solution of SDE \eqref{SDE} with initial distribution $\mu\in\sP(\mR^d)$ if $W$ is an $\sF_t$-Brownian motion, $\bP\circ X_0^{-1}=\mu$, and
\begin{align}
    X_t=X_0+A_t^b+\sqrt{2}W_t,\  t\in[0,T],\ a.s.,\no
\end{align}
where $A_t^b:=\lim_{n\to\infty}\int_0^t b_n(s,X_s)\dif s$ exists in the $L^2$-sense.

[Prop 11 of arXiv:2606.08115, '2606.08115'] Let $\ov{X}$ be a two-dimensional random vector. Then the conditions
\begin{align*}
&\ex \left|\ov{X}\right|^2 \ln \left(1 + \left|\ov{X}\right|\right) < \infty, \quad \ex \left|M\ov{X}\right|^2 \ln \left(1 + \left|M\ov{X}\right|\right) < \infty
\end{align*}
are equivalent.

[Definition d: f_r of arXiv:2605.22803, 'Persistence of asymptotic variance under transport:\\ from hyperfluctuation to stealthy hy'] Let $f:\R^d\to\C$ be a function and $r>0$. Then $f_r:\R^d\to\C$ is defined by
    \begin{equation}
        f_r(x) := f(\tfrac{x}{r}),\quad x\in\R^d.
    \end{equation}

[Proposition prop:norms_of_F_n_functions_app of arXiv:2507.22011, 'Random Lozenge Waterfall:\\Dimensional Collapse of Gibbs Measures'] For all $n\in \tfrac12 \mathbb{Z}_{\ge0}$, we have
	\begin{equation}

		\|\mathcal{F}_n\|_{\ell^2(\mathbb{Z})}^2=
		\|\mathcal{F}_n\|_{\ell^2(\mathbb{Z'})}^2=
		\frac12\|\mathcal{F}_n\|_{\ell^2(\!{\scriptscriptstyle\frac12}\mathbb{Z})}^2=
		\frac{
		(q;q)_\infty(-\kappa^2)\hspace{1pt}\theta_q(\kappa^{-2})}
		{q^n(q;q)_{2n}\hspace{1pt}\theta_{\sqrt{q}}(|\kappa|)^2}.
	\end{equation}

