[Proposition prop:harmonic of arXiv:2609.02987, 'Tail Likelihood Reinforcement Learning'] Fix an input $x$ and a parameter $\theta$.
For every truncation order $T \ge 1$,
\begin{equation}
J^{(T)}_{\mathrm{TailRL{}}}(\theta;x)
=
\sum_{k=1}^{T} \frac{1}{k}\Big(\text{\emph{Best-of-}}k(\theta;x) - 1\Big),

\end{equation}
and if $J_{\mathrm{TailRL{}}}(\theta;x) > -\infty$, the same identity holds for the full series,
\begin{equation}
J_{\mathrm{TailRL{}}}(\theta;x)
=
\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{k}\Big(\text{\emph{Best-of-}}k(\theta;x) - 1\Big).

\end{equation}

[Corollary 7 of arXiv:2609.03881, 'Eulerian insertion operators and an Eulerian form of the Pieri rule'] We have
$$\G(z)^{-1}
 =(1-\delta z)^E=
 \sum_{r\geq0}
 (-1)^r\frac{\delta^r}{r!}
 E(E-1)\cdots(E-r+1)z^r.$$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Corollary 7 (\textsf{Zero-temperature BLPP identity}) of arXiv:2609.02064, 'Coalescence and fluctuations of O'Connell--Yor polymers\\ via the free-energy correlation '] Fix $n\in\Z_{>0}$, $\al\in\R$, and $x,\wt x\in\R$ with
  $x\neq\wt x$.  Then,
  \begin{align*}
    \frac{\partial}{\partial x}
    \EE\bigl[L_{\wt x}^{\al}L_x^{\al}\bigr]
    &=
    \frac{1-\al^2}{2}
    \sgn(\wt x-x)
    \rmP_{\wt x,x}^{\al,\infty}
    (\tau\leq n-1)
  +
    \al^2
    \rmP_0^{\al,\infty}(\ga_n>-x)
    -\frac{\al^2}{2}
    \bigl(1-\sgn(\wt x-x)\bigr).
  \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Prop p:energy-level-set of arXiv:2607.18194, 'Temperature chaos in directed polymers'] There exists a deterministic sequence $\{n_k\}_{k\geqslant 1}\subset \mathbb{Z}_{\geqslant 1}$ with $n_k\uparrow\infty$ such that for any $\beta\geqslant 0$, it holds almost surely that
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb{P}_\beta^\xi\left(
            \lim_{k\to\infty}
            \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n_k} \xi_i \gamma^X_i}{\sum_{i=1}^{n_k} (\gamma^X_i)^2} = \beta
        \right)
        = 1.
    \end{align*}
    In particular, for any $\beta_1\ne \beta_2$, the CDRP measures $\mathbb{P}_{\beta_1}^\xi$ and $\mathbb{P}_{\beta_2}^\xi$ are mutually singular almost surely, as claimed in (t:pathsing of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition 182 of arXiv:2601.16377, '2601.16377'] Given a tiling of $\Omega$ and a vertex $v$ of the triangular grid inside $\Omega$, the \emph{local} height function $\mathsf{Ht}^{\textnormal{loc}}_\N(v)$ is defined by a local rule. If $u \rightarrow v$
is a positive direction, as in Figure~\ref{Figure_positive_dir}, then
\begin{equation}
  \mathsf{Ht}^\textnormal{loc}_\N(v)-\mathsf{Ht}^\textnormal{loc}_\N(u)=
  \left\{ \begin{array}{lll}
  1 & &\textnormal{if}\,\ u \rightarrow v\,\,\textnormal{is the edge of a lozenge,} \\
  -2 && \textnormal{if}\,\,u \rightarrow v\,\,\textnormal{crosses a lozenge diagonally.}
  \end{array}\right.
\end{equation}

[Lem 57 of arXiv:2510.22783, 'Universality of Cutoff for Riffle Shuffling'] With $\nu_{\Unif}$ the uniform measure on $\mathsf S_N$ and $A_{\mu,\chi,\rho,\varphi}$ the event defined in Lemma \ref{hypo-test1}, we have
        \begin{equation}
            \nu_{\Unif}(A_{\mu,\chi,\rho,\varphi})\to 0
        \end{equation}
        for any mappings $H(\cdot )$ such that $|H(\vec\bn)|\geq N^{1/2}$ and $|\partial H(\vec\bn)|=O(|H|^{1/2})$ for all $(\chi,\rho,\varphi)$-almost-$\mu$-like pile sizes $\vec\bn$.

[Proposition 4 of arXiv:2512.08595, 'Small time asymptotics of spectral heat content of isotropic processes'] For any bounded open set $\Omega$ with $C^{1,1}$ boundary we have
		\begin{align}
			\lim_{t\downarrow 0}\frac{\Vol(\Omega)-Q^X_\Omega(t)}{m(t)}=\Per(\Omega),
		\end{align}
		where $m(t)=\E_0\left[\overline{X}^{(1)}_t\right]$. In particular when $U_t$ has continuous paths, we have $m(t)=\E[\overline{Y}^{(1)}_1]\E[U^{1/\alpha}_t]$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition Fisher Chaos of arXiv:2511.05223, 'Kac's Program and Relative Entropy Decay for Nonlinear Spin-Exchange Dynamics'] Fix $h\in\G_\ensuremath{\mathcal A}$, and let $\mu:=\mu_{J,h}$. Suppose  $f\in\ensuremath{\mathcal S}_h$ is such that the  measure \(\nu := f \mu \in \mathcal{P}(\Omega)\) is irreducible. 
        Let $\r$ denote the common canonical density of $\nu$ and $\mu$, and define
    \begin{equation}
        F_N := \frac{\gamma_N(\nu)}{\gamma_N(\mu)}.
    \end{equation}
    Then,
    \begin{equation}
    \lim_{N \to \infty} \frac{1}{N}\, \mathcal{E}_{N, J,\r}(F_N, \log F_N) = 2\mathcal{D}_\mu(f).
    \end{equation}

