for any $u>0$. So in an interval $I \subset [0,T]$ of length $\leq n^{-1}$, we have $\mathbb{P}(||X(t)-X(s)||>1+u)\leq e^{-\frac{u^2}{4|t-s|}}$ for all $t,s \in I$. In particular, it follows for $u>0$ that
\begin{equation}
    \mathbb{P}(||X(t)-X(s)||>u)\leq C e^{-u^2/4},
\end{equation}
for some $C>0$. ...","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:6537a791d5448569ea39becef16367d6cc1af674ea6b2ddf93719fb0f3ae02a5","revision_digest":"sha256:2a842966fd051911b4146b1cfc2f0a4af9aec4dc8188bcd79d66a089a8cb616c","source_id":"arxiv:2411.01633","source_revision":"tex-as-downloaded-2026-09-08","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

body:
[Definition 4 (Geometric $p$-rough paths) of arXiv:2606.22964, 'Signature McKean--Vlasov stochastic differential equations'] Let $p\geq 1$. We define $C^{0,\textup{$p$-var}}_o([0,T],G^{\lfloor p\rfloor}(\mathbb{R}^k))$ as the set of continuous paths $\mathbf{x}:[0,T]\rightarrow G^{\lfloor p\rfloor}(\mathbb{R}^k)$ such that $\mathbf{x}_0=(1,0,\dots,0)$ and for which there exists a sequence of bounded variation, $\mathbb{R}^k$-valued paths $\{x_n\}_{n\geq 0}$ such that
    \begin{equation*}
        d_{\textup{hom $p$-var};[0,T]}\left(\mathbf{x},S^{\leq \lfloor p\rfloor}(x_n)_\cdot\right)\xrightarrow{n\rightarrow\infty} 0\qquad \Big(\textit{equiv.}\;d_{\textup{inhom $p$-var};[0,T]}\left(\mathbf{x},S^{\leq \lfloor p\rfloor}(x_n)_\cdot\right)\xrightarrow{n\rightarrow\infty} 0\Big),
    \end{equation*}
    where $S^{\leq \lfloor p\rfloor}_\cdot$ denotes the \emph{step-$\lfloor p\rfloor$ signature} for continuous paths of bounded variation \eqref{eq:signature_C_1_path} as a function of time.

[Defn 102 of arXiv:2510.22155, 'Random walks in space-time random media in all spatial dimensions: the full subcritical fl'] Let $k,d\in\mathbb N$. Suppose that $\br\in \Ekk$, and define 
    \begin{equation}
        \mathrm{Dr}_{\br}(\x):= \bigg|\int_{I^k} (\y-\x) \br(\x,\dr\y) - \mathbf s(\nu_{\br})\bigg|.
    \end{equation}
    Note that this is 0 for any centered chain $\br$. 
    Here $\mathbf s(\nu_{\br}) = \int_{I^k} \mathbf u \nu_{\br} (\dr u)$ is the deterministic drift vector as used in Theorem \ref{inv00}.

[Proposition 49 (Scaling of white noise) of arXiv:2605.14630, '2605.14630'] For any $\lambda\in(0,1]$, one has equality in law 
\begin{equation}
 \xi_\lambda \eqinlaw \frac{1}{\lambda^{d/2}} \xi\;.

\end{equation}

