[Lem lemma:k_th_moment of arXiv:2509.06730, 'Hyperbolic branching Brownian motion: \\ the empirical limit measure'] For any $\beta>0$, any $K>0$, any $k\in \ensuremath{\mathbb{N}}_{\geq 2}$, and any $\delta>0$, there is $C_3 =C_3(\beta, K, k, \delta)< \infty$ such that
    \begin{equation*}
        \limsup_{\ensuremath{\varepsilon} \to 0} \sup_{\substack{I\subseteq \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}} \\ \vert I \vert =\ensuremath{\varepsilon}}} \frac{\ensuremath{\mathbb{E}}\left[ \mu^K_\infty(I)^k\right]}{\ensuremath{\varepsilon}^{k \wedge(1+2\beta (k-1)-\delta)}} \leq C_3<\infty.
    \end{equation*}

