[Theorem thrm:examples of arXiv:2411.18749, 'A star is born: Explosive Crump--Mode--Jagers branching processes'] Consider the four examples $(i)$-$(iv)$. The infinite tree $T_\infty$ almost surely contains a unique infinite star and no infinite path when
	\begin{equation}
	(i)\ \  (\sigma-1)\kappa>1;\qquad (ii)\ \  \nu\gamma>1;\qquad (iii)\ \ (\sigma-1)\kappa>1; \qquad (iv)\ \  (\sigma-1)\kappa>1.
	\end{equation}
	When the above inequalities are reversed $($i.e.\ when changing the $>$ to a $<)$, almost surely $T_\infty$ contains a unique infinite path and no infinite star. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

