{"artifact_digest":"sha256:c7e873267872f41d2700942ebff09edbf19cc1bd3ae3a585e85d5e726c597a29","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/c7/c7e873267872f41d2700942ebff09edbf19cc1bd3ae3a585e85d5e726c597a29.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:bc73fbcccfabb39eecda16b2907ea40bfbf4364521e850944d12149b4f92920d","bundle_entry_key":"2606-13487-theorem-3-tightness","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_049","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_049.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2606-13487-propn-3-tightness","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: By Theorem 1.18, \cite{etheridge}, in order to show tightness, it is sufficient to show the following conditions:
    \begin{enumerate}[noitemsep]
        \item[(i)] \textit{Compact containment:} For all $\epsilon > 0$ there exists a compact subset $K_\epsilon\subseteq \C{M}_F^w(\bar{\B{R}})$ such that $$\inf_{N\in \B{N}} \B{P}(\mu_t^N \in K_\epsilon\;  \forall \, t\in [0,T])>1-\epsilon.$$
