[Theorem thm:PLR of arXiv:2609.00794, 'On operators whose adjoints or second adjoints attain their norms'] {Oja}}] Let $X$ and $Y$ be Banach spaces, and let $S \in \mathcal{F}(X^{**},Y^{**})$. If $E$ and $F$ are finite-dimensional subspaces of $X^{**}$ and $Y^*$, respectively, and $\varepsilon>0$, then there exists $T \in \mathcal{F}(X,Y)$ such that 
	\begin{enumerate}
		\itemsep0.25em
		\item $|\|T\|-\|S\||<\varepsilon$;
		\item $x^{**} (T^* y^*) = (Sx^{**} )(y^*)$ for all $x^{**} \in E$ and $y^* \in F$;
		\item $T^{**} x^{**} = S x^{**}$ for all $x^{**} \in E$ with $Sx^{**} \in Y$.
	\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 28 of arXiv:2604.11682, 'Semilocalization for inhomogeneous random graphs'] Let $\nu > 0$. With $\nu$-high probability, for all $x \in \hcV^{(\rm h)}_{\nu}$ that
