[Proposition 3 (Derivative of entropy dissipation) of arXiv:2609.03847, 'A counterexample to McKean's conjecture for the Landau-Coulomb equation'] Let $f$ be a classical solution of the Landau-Coulomb equation~\eqref{eq:Landau_Coulomb}. Then
	\begin{equation}
		\frac{\d}{\d t}D_H(f)
		=\int_{\R^3}Q(f,f)\,\delta_{f}\d v	-2\int_{\R^3}\frac{Q(f,f)^2}{f}\d v	,
	\end{equation}
where $\delta_f$ is given by
\begin{align}
	 \delta_f(v) \coloneqq 	\int_{\R^3} 	f_* \frac{	\left| 	\Pi_{v-v_*} ^\perp	\bigl(\nabla\log f-\nabla_*\log f_*\bigr)\right|^2}{|v-v_*|} 	\d v_*.
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition 11 (Master equation for the eigenvectors) of arXiv:2511.12623, 'The eigenvector bead process'] Assume the spectrum $\{\lambda^{(N)}_j\}_{j=1}^N$ is simple.
Let $\lambda^{(N+1)}_i$ be any zero of \eqref{eq:secular}. Then the overlaps satisfy
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
\Omega^{(N)}_{i,j}
&= \frac{1}{\sqrt{\bigl|f'(\lambda^{(N+1)}_i)\bigr|}}\,
   \frac{g_j^{(N)}}{\Delta_{i,j}^{(N)}},
\qquad j=1,\dots,N, \\[4pt]
f'(\lambda)
&= -\left( 1 + \sum_{j=1}^N
           \left(\frac{|g_j^{(N)}|}{\lambda-\lambda^{(N)}_j}\right)^{\!2} \right).

\end{align}
\end{subequations} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

