  \begin{align*}
    B(t,a) \ge \int_0^1 \ensuremath{\mathrm{d} } \tau\, |\tau|^{1-2H} \left|\int_0^t \ensuremath{\mathrm{d} } s \, e^{i\tau s} \int_0^s \ensuremath{\mathrm{d} } r \, (s-r)^{\gamma-1}r\,\frac{\sin(ar)}{ar} \right|^2,
  \end{align*}
  we can use $\sin(x)/x \to 1$ as $x \to 0$ and the dominated convergence
  theorem to deduce that there exists $c>0$ and $\delta_0>0$ such that $B(t,a)
    \ge c$ for all $a\in(0,\delta_0)$.

  Next, consider $a \ge \delta_0$. By scaling, we can write
  \begin{align}
    B(t,a) = a^{-2\gamma-2H-2} \underbrace{\int_\mathbb{R} \ensuremath{\mathrm{d} } \tau \, |\tau|^{1-2H} \left| \int_0^{ta} \ensuremath{\mathrm{d} } s \, e^{i\tau s} ...","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:b8af051ae60512de37ad97dde87d4fb94a99b60d24706ebd79ed4c4d01b70230","revision_digest":"sha256:3b1af3db17f05c0aadb16d6ce9fe6455663ebfe74b4ca0e5ef8bac0fb00a71dd","source_id":"arxiv:2602.05317","source_revision":"tex-as-downloaded-2026-09-08","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

