body:
[Prop prop_iii of arXiv:2608.30382, 'Convergence rates for the RMSprop optimizer with full control of the hyperparameters'] Let $\beta\in[ \nicefrac{1}{2}, 1 ) $,
$ \varepsilon \in [0,\infty) $,
$ i \in \{ 1, 2, \dots, d \} $,
$ n \in {\mathbb N} \backslash \{ 1 \} $,
let $ A_n \in \mathcal F_{ n - 1 } $,
and let $ C_X^{ (3) } , C_v^{ (-3) } \in (0,\infty) $.
Assume that
\bas{
  {\mathbb E}\bigl[
    | X^{(i)}(\theta_{n-1},U_n) |^3
    |
    \mathcal F_{ n - 1 }
  \bigr]
  \le C_X^{ (3) }
\qquad\text{on }A_n
}
and
\bas{

  {\mathbb E}[ \1_{ A_n }( v_{ n - 1 }^{ (i) } )^{ - 3 } ]
  \le C_v^{ ( - 3 ) }
  .
}
The negative-moment assumption implies that $v_{n-1}^{(i)}>0$ almost surely
on $A_n$. If $\varepsilon=0$, the conditional covariances in the conclusion are
therefore evaluated on $A_n$ and extended by zero to $ \Omega \backslash A_n $.
Then one has
\bas{

  {\mathbb E}\Bigl[
    \1_{A_n}\mathrm{cov}((\sqrt{v_n^{(i)}}+\varepsilon)^{-1},
    X^{(i)}(\theta_{n-1},U_n)|\mathcal F_{n-1})^2
  \Bigr]^{1/2}
  \le
  4 C_X^{(3)} (C_v^{(-3)})^{1/2}\,\frac{1-\beta}{1-\beta^{n-1}}.
}

metadata_json:
{"artifact_digest":"sha256:1530b0c8ed0bd401996aad7456a71bc0e646c2515d85bddaa41aecb508c9d76c","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/15/1530b0c8ed0bd401996aad7456a71bc0e646c2515d85bddaa41aecb508c9d76c.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:923847a4bdd09ce28c5f05d7a43a8b80028eb0706ca64b936e6d464e31a86004","bundle_entry_key":"2608-30382-theorem-3-prop-iii","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s1_068","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s1_068.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2608-30382-prop-3-prop-iii","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Set
\bas{
Y_n^{(i)}=\frac{\beta(1-\beta^{n-1})}{1-\beta^n}v_{n-1}^{(i)}
=\kappa_n v_{n-1}^{(i)}
\qquad
  \text{and}
\qquad
  Z_n=\frac{1-\beta}{1-\beta^n}.
}
Then
\bas{
v_n^{(i)}=Y_n^{(i)}+Z_nX^{(i)}(\theta_{n-1},U_n)^2.
}
The negative-moment assumption gives $v_{n-1}^{(i)}>0$ almost surely on
$A_n$, and hence $Y_n^{(i)}>0$ there. In what follows, all conditional
identities involving inverse factors are understood on $A_n$.
The function 
\bas{
h:(0,\infty)\to(0,\infty), \ y\mapsto h(y)=\frac1{\sqrt y+\varepsilon}
