[Proposition 37 (Commuting pairs do not suffice) of arXiv:2609.03736, '2609.03736'] If $K$ and $L$ commute, then
\begin{equation}
 J(K,L)\leq\frac58<\frac2\pi.

\end{equation}
More precisely, after simultaneous diagonalization, put
\begin{equation}
 s_i=k_i+l_i,\qquad
 p_i=\frac{\sqrt{k_il_i}}{s_i}

\end{equation}
on the indices for which $s_i>0$.  Then
\begin{equation}
 \frac58-J(K,L)
 =2\sum_i s_i\left(p_i-\frac14\right)^2.

\end{equation}

[Defi def:SDE of arXiv:2509.08659, 'Gap metrics for stationary point processes and quantitative convexity of the free energy'] For probability measures $\Pi$ on $[0,\infty)^I$, $I\subset \IZ$, we let $\mathsf{S}_t^{gap}\Pi:=\mathsf{law}(X_t)$, where $(X_t)_{t\geq 0}$ is  the unique weak  solution (cf.\ [pilipenko]) of the SDE \begin{align}
        dX^i_t=-dt+l^i_t+\sqrt{2}dB^i_t,\quad i\in I,
    \end{align}
    with $\mathsf{law}(X_0)=\Pi$ where
  $(B^i)_{i\in I}$ are independent standard Brownian motions and $(l^i_t)_{t\geq 0}$ is the local time of $X^i$ in $0$ for $i\in I$.

[Thm of arXiv:2608.29013, 'Weighted Homology and Cohomology of Weighted Polyhedra'] Let $(X, \lambda)$ be a descending type weighted polyhedron. 
     For any cohomology classes $[\phi]\in \mathcal{H}^p_W(X, \lambda)$ and $[\psi]\in \mathcal{H}^q_W(X, \lambda)$, we have 
     \begin{equation}
         [\phi] \Cup [\psi] = (-1)^{pq} [\psi] \Cup [\phi]. 
     \end{equation}

