[Prop eq:taum of arXiv:2603.21634, 'Individual-based stochastic model with unbounded growth, birth and death rates: a tightnes'] Under the \hyperlink{gen}{general setting}, we almost surely have
\begin{align}
\tau_{M} \xrightarrow[M \rightarrow + \infty]{} + \infty.

\end{align} 
Then, for all $T \geq 0$, 
\begin{align}
\mathbb{E}\left(\sup\limits_{t \in [0,T \wedge J_{\infty})} (E_{t}+N_{t}+\Omega_{t})\right) < + \infty.

\end{align}
This immediately implies that for every $\varphi \in \mathfrak{B}_{1+\mathrm{Id}+\omega}(\mathbb{R}^{*}_{+})$, we have
\[\mathbb{E}\left(\underset{t \in [0,T \wedge J_{\infty})}{\mathrm{sup}} |\langle \mu_{t}, \varphi \rangle| \right) < + \infty. \] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition def_moving_Lambda of arXiv:2504.00512, 'Localization-delocalization transition for a random block matrix model at the edge'] Suppose $\Lambda_t:[a, b] \rightarrow \mathbb{C}^{D N \times D N}$ is a continuous map such that $\Lambda_t$ satisfies (main_assm of the paper) throughout the evolution. 
     We define $m_t(z)$ as the unique solution to 
 \begin{equation}\nonumber
m_t(z)= \big\langle \left(\Lambda_t -z- m_t(z)\right)^{-1} \big\rangle ,
\end{equation}
such that $\im m_t(z)>0$ whenever $z\in \mathbb{C}_+$.  

Then, we define $M_t=M_t\left({z,\Lambda_t}\right)$ as
\begin{equation}\nonumber
        \begin{aligned}
            M_t(z)=
            \left(\Lambda_t-z-m_t(z)\right)^{-1},
        \end{aligned}
    \end{equation}
noting that $m_t(z)=\left\langle M_t(z)\right\rangle$. 

The associated probability density is given by
    \begin{equation}\nonumber
        \begin{aligned}
            \rho_t(E)=\frac{1}{\pi} \lim _{\eta \downarrow 0} \operatorname{Im} m_t(E+i \eta).
        \end{aligned}
    \end{equation}
We denote the support of $\rho_t$ by $\left[E_t^{-}, E_t^{+}\right]$, where $E_t^{\pm}$ represent the spectral edges. For $z=E+\mathrm{i} \eta$, we also define $ \kappa_t\equiv \kappa_t(E):=|E_t^{+}-E|\wedge |E-E_t^{-}|$. Finally, we define the quantiles $\gamma_k\left({t}\right)$ of $\rho_t$ as in (equation eq:gammak of the paper).

[Prop two-pt classification half-space of arXiv:2608.18045, 'Supercritical sharpness for the random cluster representation of real-valued spin models'] Let $(\beta,h)\in (\mathbb R^+)^2$. Assume that for every pair of neighbours $u,v\in \mathbb H$,
\begin{equation}
    \langle \tau_u\tau_v\rangle_{\mathbb H,\beta}^{+,h}=\langle \tau_u\tau_v\rangle_{\mathbb H,\beta}^{0,h}.
\end{equation}
Then,
\begin{equation}
    \Psi^{1,h}_{\mathbb H,\beta}=\Psi^{0,h}_{\mathbb H,\beta}.
\end{equation}

