\item The $(\om,\eta)$-marginal of $R^{\Lambda, -t, -s}(\tau)$ will be denoted by $R_{(\om,\tau)}^{\Lambda, -t, -s}(\tau)$.
\item The $\alpha$-marginal of $R^{\Lambda, -t, -s}(\tau)$ will be denoted by $R_\alpha^{\Lambda, -t, -s}(\tau)$. 
\end{itemize}

[Definition s-normalization of arXiv:2412.08400, '\vspace{-1.0cm} Characterization of Exponential Families of \\ Lumpable Stochastic Matrice'] When $(\mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{E})$ is strongly connected we define the mapping
\begin{equation*}
\begin{split}
    {\mathfrak{s}} \colon \mathcal{F}^+(\mathcal{Y},\mathcal{E}) &\to \mathcal{W}(\mathcal{Y},\mathcal{E}) \\
    F &\mapsto P \colon \mathcal{E} \to \mathbb{R}_+, (y,y') \mapsto P(y,y') = \frac{F(y,y') v_F(y')}{\rho_F v_F(y)},
\end{split}
\end{equation*}    
where $\rho_F$ and $v_F$ are respectively the Perron--Frobenius (PF) root and associated right eigenvector of $F$. Henceforth, $(\rho_F, v_F)$ will be called the right PF eigen-pair of $F$.

