[Lemma 39 (Traces on timelike hypersurfaces) of arXiv:2608.31171, '\bf Finite-energy spacetimes with torus symmetry. \\ \Large Cauchy stability of Einstein--'] Let $I=(t_0,t_1)$ and let $A,B\in L^1(I\times\Sbb^1)$ satisfy
\bel{eq:rect-bal}
\del_tA+\del_xB=\mu
\ee
in distributions, where $\mu$ is a finite Radon measure. For every $\varphi\in C_c^1(I)$, the function
\bel{eq:rect-Bphi}
x\longmapsto B_\varphi(x)
\coloneqq
\int_I B(t,x)\varphi(t)\,dt
\ee
has a $\BV(\Sbb^1)$ representative. Its precise representative defines a distributional normal trace $\operatorname{Tr}_xB$ on every timelike line $x=\mathrm{const}$. If $B\geq0$, these traces are non-negative Radon measures in~$t$. If, in addition, $A$ has the one-sided traces on spacelike hypersurfaces supplied by \autoref{prop-weak-integrated-current}, then
\bel{eq:rect-trace-bound}
\sup_{x\in\Sbb^1}\|\operatorname{Tr}_xB\|_{\Meas(I)}
\leq C\left(
\|B\|_{L^1(I\times\Sbb^1)}
+\|A(t_0+)\|_{L^1(\Sbb^1)}
+\|A(t_1-)\|_{L^1(\Sbb^1)}
+|\mu|(I\times\Sbb^1)
\right),
\ee
where $C$ depends only on the period of the spatial circle. The same conclusion holds for a weighted balance law after its positive weights have been included in $A$ and~$B$.

[Theorem hydrodynamics of arXiv:2412.04015, 'Linear fluctuation of interfaces in Glauber-Kawasaki dynamics'] Fix a measurable function $\rho \colon {\mathbb T}^d\to[0,1]$.  Let $\nu_N$
be a sequence of product probability measures on $\Omega_N$ associated
to $\rho$, in the sense that
\begin{equation*}
\lim_{N\to\infty}
\nu_N \Big(\, \big |\langle\pi^{N},G\rangle -\int_{{\mathbb T}^d}
G(\theta)\, \rho(\theta)\, d\theta\,\big|>\delta\, \Big)\;=\;0\;,
\end{equation*}
for every $\delta>0$ and every continuous function $G\colon {\mathbb T}^d\to{\mathbb{R}}$.  Then,
for every $t\ge 0$, every $\delta>0$ and every continuous function
$G:{\mathbb T}^d\to{\mathbb{R}}$, 
\begin{equation*}
\lim_{N\to\infty}
\mathbb{P}_{\nu_N}
\Big(\, \big |\langle\pi^{N}_t ,G\rangle -\int_{{\mathbb T}^d}
G(\theta)\, u(t,\theta)\, d\theta\,\big|>\delta\, \Big) \;=\;0\;,
\end{equation*}
where $u\colon [0,\infty)\times{\mathbb T}^d\to[0,1]$ is the unique weak solution of
the Cauchy problem
\begin{equation}
\begin{cases}
     \partial_tu \;=\; \Delta u \,-\, K\, V'(u) \ \text{ on }\ {\mathbb T}^d\;,\\
     u(0,\cdot)\;=\;\rho (\cdot)\;,
\end{cases}

\end{equation}
and $V'(\cdot)$ is given by (equation 2-54 of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem Thm:exact of arXiv:2411.13030, 'Classification of the limit shape for 1+1-dimensional FPP'] Let $p\in [0,1]$ and $\kappa > \lambda \ge 0$. Let each edge $e$ be independently distributed according to
