[Definition def:six-observables of arXiv:2605.17082, 'Spectral Purification in Reversible Markov Chains: Hidden Parameters, Observable Equivalen'] With $u_k := v_k$ the normalized power-iteration vector of (sec:power-iteration of the paper) and $\rho_k := E_{k+1}/E_k$ the energy retention ratio of (thm:observable-variance of the paper), define
\begin{align*}
G_k &:= \|u_k - \operatorname{sign}(c_2)\phi_2\|_\pi^2, & &\text{(geometric/iterate error)}\\
I_k &:= 1 - \langle u_k, \phi_2\rangle_\pi, & &\text{(direction deficit)}\\
R_k &:= \langle u_k, P u_k\rangle_\pi - \lambda_2, & &\text{(Rayleigh quotient error)}\\
\Lambda_k &:= \sqrt{E_{k+1}/E_k} - \lambda_2, & &\text{(eigenvalue estimate error)}\\
\Gamma_k &:= \rho_{k+1}/\rho_k - 1, & &\text{(energy ratio increment)}\\
\mathcal{H}_k &:= \frac{H(k)}{2 L_\star k}, & &\text{(normalized spectral entropy)}
\end{align*}
where $H(k) = -\sum_{i\ge2} p_i(k)\log p_i(k)$ is the spectral entropy of (sec:thermodynamics of the paper).

[Theorem thm:conc_current of arXiv:2609.01581, 'Concentration of additive functionals of Stratonovich-type'] Let $(X_t)_{t \geq 0}$ be an ergodic diffusion with invariant measure $\mu$, let
$\overline{J}_t(U)$ be the empirical current in the sense of Definition~(def:empirical_current of the paper) with 
ergodic mean $\E^\mu[\overline{J}_t] = \int j_{\textrm{s}} \cdot U \mathrm{d} x$, and let $\nu \ll \mu$
with $\mathrm{d}\nu/\mathrm{d}\mu \in L^2(\mu)$. Then for all $t > 0$ and $a > 0$,
\begin{align}
  \mathbb{P}^\nu\left(\overline{J}_t(U) - \E^\mu[\overline{J}_t] \geq a\right)
  \leq
  \left\|\frac{\mathrm{d}\nu}{\mathrm{d}\mu}\right\|_{L^2(\mu)}
  \exp\bigl[-tI^J\bigl(\E^\mu[\overline{J}_t] + a\bigr)\bigr],

\end{align}
where the Cram\'er transform
\begin{align}
  I^J(s) \equiv \sup_{k \in \mathbb{R}}\bigl[k s - \Lambda^J(k)\bigr],

\end{align}
is defined as the Legendre conjugate of the principal eigenvalue $\Lambda^J(k)$~(equation eq:Lambda_J_dirichlet of the paper).

[Lemma 8 (H\"older / log-convexity) of arXiv:2604.13149, 'The Distributional Tail of Worst--Case Quickselect'] For every \(n\ge 0\), every \(\theta> 0\), and every \(u\in[0,1]\),
\begin{equation}
\psi_n(\theta u)\le \psi_n(\theta)^u.
\end{equation}

