body:
[Proposition prop:nonuniform:CLT:crude of arXiv:2607.04047, 'Top Singular Value in Sum-Products of Random Matrices'] There exists some universal constant $a > 0$ such that 
\begin{align*}
    {\mathbb P}( E_1 > x ) 
    \;=&\;
    \Phi^c(x) 
     \,
     \Big( 1 + O\Big( \mfrac{1}{n} + \mfrac{N^{1/2}}{n^{3/2}}  + \mfrac{1+|x|^3}{\sqrt{N}}\Big) \Big)
    \qquad 
    \text{ for } 
    \quad 
    0 \leq x \leq a N^{1/6}
    \;,
    \\
    {\mathbb P}( E_1 < x ) 
    \;=&\;
    \Phi(x) 
     \,
     \Big( 1 + O\Big( \mfrac{1}{n} + \mfrac{N^{1/2}}{n^{3/2}}  + \mfrac{1+|x|^3}{\sqrt{N}}\Big) \Big)
    \qquad 
    \text{ for } 
    \quad 
    - a N^{1/6} \leq x \leq 0
    \;.
\end{align*}
Suppose in addition that $|x| = o(N^{1/6})$ and $N=o(n^3)$. If $x > 0$ with $x = \omega(1)$, then 
\begin{align*}
    {\mathbb P}( E_1 > x ) 
    \;=\; 
    (1+o(1)) \, e^{ - \frac{x^2}{2} + o(x) }
    \;.
\end{align*}
If $x < 0$ with $|x| = \omega(1)$, then
\begin{align*}
    {\mathbb P}( E_1 < x ) 
    \;=\; 
    (1+o(1)) \, e^{ - \frac{x^2}{2} + o(|x|) }
    \;.
\end{align*}
If instead $x=O(1)$, we have 
\begin{align*}
    {\mathbb P}( E_1 > x ) 
    \;=&\;
    \begin{cases}
        \Theta( e^{-x^2/2} ) 
        & 
        \text{ if } x > 0\;,
        \\
        \frac{1}{2} (1 + o(1))
        & 
        \text{ if } x = o(1)\;,
        \\
        1 - \Theta( e^{-x^2/2} ) 
        & 
        \text{ if } x < 0\;.
    \end{cases}
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)
